window.harnessChunks["rows-c98e703f37eb06b2.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T13:59:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/smartcomputer-ai/lightspeed\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Lightspeed</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/smartcomputer-ai/lightspeed/tree/2873e77dca4325aac116b81db2b520502c037ac4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2873e77dca4325aac116b81db2b520502c037ac4\">2873e77 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Interpret incoming work and execute model-directed actions in a durable bot session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unpredictable external events, files and tool results, model/provider failures, tasks with open-ended next steps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select tools, read event details, invoke subagents or external actions and choose the event disposition. - Decision owner: First-party model-facing agent loop within each configured Lightspeed bot session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Event-sourced reducer, Temporal recovery, CAS references, tool registry, one-active-run admission, profile grants, tools and fixed budget controls. - Closure path: Event starts session run, model reads evidence and calls tools, effects are committed and returned for next model step; boteventresolve records the agent&#39;s decision. - Boundary reachability: The documented local stack starts session and bot workers; a created bot gets Main and configured routed sessions with model, standing brief and event tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The LLM chooses meaningful event actions and outcome, whereas the workflow merely preserves and executes their effects. - Evidence: core agent drive and durable loop, bot instructions/outcome tool, bot event guide. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: No external provider or deployed fleet was run in this source review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No function-specific agent-owned or constructor-complete inter-S1 conflict regulator is established for this bot&#39;s multiple sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Busy-session delivery, repeated events and load bursts are attenuated mechanically; no separately evidenced interference among distinct viable operational sessions is resolved. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A configured trigger picks queue/steer/append/coalesce, but this ordinarily changes admission to a target session rather than resolving a conflict among distinct S1 cells. - Decision owner: Operator/model can set trigger policies; deterministic bot controller dispatches and deduplicates, without a verified distinct inter-S1 conflict decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Event ids, CEL route keys, busy delivery modes, coalescing, rate/breaker limits, federation receipts and subagent limits. - Closure path: Buffered events are eventually delivered; a sender may get a receipt, but no source witness ties this ordinary traffic to a corrective decision between separately operating S1 cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A message bus, per-session queue, default scheduler and delegation are not alone S2, even though their safety/throughput roles are useful. - Evidence: controller lanes and state, event route policies, federation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A configured concrete multi-bot workplace may add inter-S1 interference regulation; generic interoperability is not counted.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Oversee the current standing bot&#39;s event/session portfolio, admission constraints, work intake and bot-level interventions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Repeated failed events, flooded inbox, multiple active delivery threads, budget exhaustion and mismatched routing or work cadence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In selfConfig mode the Main model can inspect all current sessions, recent/active deliveries, buffers and run usage, then revise trigger routes, filters, cadence and busy-session policies. Human Operator can additionally alter whole-bot run budgets, pause/replay/reset and related current commitments. - Decision owner: Model in the enabled selfConfig Main mode (A); legitimate human Operator/Admin controlling the bot&#39;s full settings and Activity (P). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: botstatus, boteventlist/read, bottriggerput/delete, controller config signals, run budgets, breaker, session status and role-checked Platform actions. - Closure path: Current bot status exposes overloaded/failed work; model/operator revises trigger/control settings; API validates them; subsequent event admission and routed-session operation change. - Boundary reachability: Bot tools are declared in the shipped selfConfig mode and re-checked against the live grant; bot controller picks up configuration. Operator/Admin bot settings and Activity exist in shipped web/UI and core API. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model selects intake/priority constraints from whole-bot feedback in the autonomous mode; scheduler executes them. Separately, the authorized operator selects broader intervention and resource limits, so the parent mode is real rather than inferred from a pause button alone. - Whole-system current view: botstatus includes sessions, their busy states, active/recent deliveries, buffers, processed events, daily runs and breaker; Operator Activity supplies the same run/outcome context across the bot. - Current-control decision scope: Change which incoming events are filtered, routed, batched or queued and when future deliveries start; in parent mode also daily resource limit, pause, event replay and session reset. - Evidence: botstatus view, self-configuration tool declarations, tool execution and grant checks, operations and bot management, Operator role. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium for autonomous S3; high for parent bot management closure. - Caveats: The autonomous mode requires selfConfig granted by an Operator; the model cannot directly widen limits or reconfigure a different bot. Adjusting one ordinary session tool call is not S3.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No separate independent complementary S3 audit is wired to challenge ordinary bot operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect event handling, misleading tool results or missing outcomes can be observed, but no separate challenge authority is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Activity, session transcripts and event outcomes are accessible; ordinary user/agent review does not create an independent audit verdict. - Decision owner: Bot model for normal event resolution, operator for routine investigation; optional reviewer subagents remain delegated operational checks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Event-sourced history, workflow logs, subagent transcripts, receipt records and event replay. - Closure path: Logs/status assist current troubleshooting; no independent sampled reality channel with a corrective audit judgment is guaranteed to reach S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Replay, normal QA and status dashboards are evidence-supporting infrastructure, not an independently governed S3 path. - Evidence: subagent reviews and limits, Activity/events, durable log. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A user may attach a truly separate auditor bot, but it is not part of the base bot boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Revise future environmental sensing and standing bot action opportunities in response to external observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Newly relevant external signals, stale polling/routing assumptions, changing schedules and information sources. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A persistent bot model can read incoming external events and current trigger configuration, then select and install a new or revised schedule, poll, webhook, filter or route to govern future work. - Decision owner: Model in the first-party selfConfig bot mode; operator initially controls that grant and can override the setup. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: boteventread, bottriggerlist, bottriggerput/delete, polling cursors, schedule reconciliation, server-validated filters and controller. - Closure path: External event/evidence changes the model&#39;s judgment of what should be watched next; a prospective trigger option becomes persisted configuration and later yields changed work admission and present S3 control context. - Boundary reachability: SelfConfig bot tools are real admitted workflow bindings; puttrigger calls the core service and schedules are reconciled into the running controller. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic polling does not choose which future signals matter; the self-configured bot model can decide the changed future monitoring and enact it. - External distinction: New webhook, monitored HTTP/command output or peer event represents a changing outside system, not internal task-memory alone. - Future / prospective distinction: A rewritten future polling schedule, source/filter or alert routing applies to later events rather than completing only the present response. - Adaptation option generated: Model-authored new/modified watch trigger, cadence, filter and routing policy. - Path back into current capability / S3: bottriggerput persists and reconciles the trigger so subsequent event arrivals alter the S3 work portfolio and S1 sessions. - Evidence: bot trigger tools and descriptions, puttrigger implementation, polling/schedules and external feeds. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Requires an enabled selfConfig grant and a model choosing prospective changes; static operator-configured polling by itself would not be autonomous S4.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Retain legitimate human authority over the standing bot&#39;s job/identity, capability grants and final purpose changes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The bot&#39;s standing brief or authorized role may no longer match human institutional purpose or risk boundaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A human Operator/Admin can redefine the bot&#39;s standing job and authorized profile and self-configuration permissions after examining outcomes, including disallowing further self-revision. - Decision owner: Legitimate human Operator/Admin within the bot&#39;s universe; bot model cannot widen its own capabilities or override operator policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bot Job/Settings, role-gated bot document update, immutable bot ID, profile grants, live selfConfig and emit permission rechecks, controller idle-boundary reapplication. - Closure path: Operator recognizes a standing-purpose or authority conflict from the bot&#39;s Activity/conversation; revises Job brief/profile/grant; controller re-applies standing instructions and permissions; future operations follow the new identity decision. - Boundary reachability: Operator/Admin bot edits are admitted through role-checked Platform/core API and bot configuration reconciliation; subsequent Main sessions use the modified standing brief/profile/permissions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This credit is to the legitimate parent human&#39;s identity-level decision and its enforceable return, not to the existence of prompt text or an approval of one tool. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Whether this persistent bot should keep its ongoing job, change its standing instructions or retain self-configuration/federation authority in its universe. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Operator/Admin for the scoped bot and universe; no autonomous S5 right is claimed merely from botbriefput. - Return-to-operation path: Bot document/config update and session reconcile at idle change the instructions/grants for subsequent routed work. - Evidence: bot job and settings lifecycle, human permission hierarchy, bot document, controller config reconcile. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Routine message approval or role check is not S5; this parent mapping applies to a bot-wide standing mission/authority revision, not ordinary event handling or the entire platform.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b9b06fce8c71a0d48bccddb1a35972270a3b3792/assessments/li/lightspeed.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b9b06fce8c71a0d48bccddb1a35972270a3b3792\">b9b06fc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T11:17:01+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/peakflo/20x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">20x</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/peakflo/20x/tree/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01\">168693b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement real task artifacts through a chosen coding model in a task workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertain requirements, source changes, failed commands and tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose actual file edits, tests, tool actions and correction attempts. - Decision owner: model-backed Claude/OpenCode/Codex/Cursor/Pi/ACP task worker, not the desktop scheduler. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent adapter, task prompt, MCP, worktree, transcript, checkpoint and status DB. - Closure path: task brief → model tool calls on repository → changed artifacts and evidence → task ready-for-review. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the deterministic Electron app cannot choose code-specific corrections without the external model. - Evidence: https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/agent-manager.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/adapters/coding-agent-adapter.ts - Basis: shipped task-agent execution path; external provider was not run. - Confidence: high for structural decision ownership, not measured outcome quality. - Caveats: human-only tasks and provider engine internals are outside the credited operating mode. - Boundary reachability: standard local task launch with a configured supported agent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no evidenced specific cross-worker interference repair in shipped task/subtask management. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two sibling tasks may duplicate or incompatibly edit a shared source interface. - Decisive decision or feedback right: parent model can inspect sibling state but no specific cross-S1 conflict judgment is required or returned. - Decision owner: parent task model owns decomposition; no qualifying inter-S1 coordination decision owner shown. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child status, worktree isolation, sibling summaries, wait/resume tools. - Closure path: subtask reports flow into parent prompt, without a demonstrated collision-specific correction loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: general instruction to coordinate and shared status do not establish actual inter-S1 variety damping. - Evidence: https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/agent-manager.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/mcp-servers/task-management-core.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/worktree-manager.ts - Basis: bounded source negative across sibling prompt, subtask wait, worktree and parent notification. - Confidence: medium; models could voluntarily reconcile specific conflicts, but not proven as a shipped mode. - Caveats: no claim that sibling workers never coordinate in a user-specific deployment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current task-portfolio assignments and priority/resource fit under a selected model-backed Mastermind/triage mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing current tasks and uneven provider subscription quotas, skill fit and agent workloads. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses suitable agent, priority, skills, repository and children from live agent usage and task evidence. - Decision owner: triage/Mastermind model at current portfolio boundary; operator retains higher authority for risky commands. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listtasks, agent workload statistics, listagents usage limits, updatetask, createsubtask, start/stop tool gates. - Closure path: live portfolio and resource data → model allocation judgment → task DB assignment and priority → subsequent worker launch. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fit and priority selection depend on a model comparing work and headroom; tools only execute the choices. - Evidence: https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/agent-manager.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/mcp-servers/task-management-core.ts - Basis: first-party model triage instructions plus writable current task-management API. - Confidence: medium for selected on-demand whole-board mode; not continuous global executive autonomy. - Caveats: single-ticket delegation alone would not suffice; positive mapping uses portfolio-wide task and agent usage information. - Whole-system current view: Mastermind can list all tasks, current statuses, priorities and agent workloads; triage can compare all available agent headroom. - Current-control decision scope: reprioritize or reassign live tasks and pick resource/skill/repository commitments; no S4 strategy or S5 policy power. - Boundary reachability: desktop Mastermind/triage launches with built-in task-management MCP and model-backed session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge task ready-for-review claims against later real PR, CI and linked-issue state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new requested changes, failing CI or external issue drift after the task agent&#39;s last report. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate heartbeat model decides whether new external findings are actionable. - Decision owner: fresh heartbeat-specific model session, distinct from original task implementer session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: heartbeat.md, deterministic gh preflight, periodic timer, dedupe and authorization gate. - Closure path: task status/checklist → current external checks → model diagnoses drift → findings forwarded to task agent or to human for authorization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model interprets changed real-world state and requests correction; preflight and forwarding are deterministic controls. - Evidence: https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/heartbeat-scheduler.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/agent-manager.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/shared/system-authority.ts - Basis: implemented separate session/current-state review and returned finding path; no live provider run. - Confidence: medium-high when opt-in monitoring is active. - Caveats: heartbeat requires a useful checklist and enabled task; this is no independent auditor organization. - Claim being audited: task agent&#39;s ready-for-review state implies no unresolved relevant linked PR/CI/issue updates. - Ordinary reporting path: task agent transcript, completion result and status. - Complementary access path: later model session polls current GitHub PR/CI/issue state against authored monitoring checklist. - Independence boundary: independent session and later external access, possibly same underlying provider. - Who acts on findings: task agent receives authorized corrective input; privileged actions are escalated to the human. - Boundary reachability: shipped HeartbeatScheduler auto-enables a new substantive heartbeat.md and runs for eligible ready-for-review tasks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no future external environment option-development loop is coupled to present S3 capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new tools and provider conditions plus changing user needs could require adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user chooses capabilities and model handles immediate task setup, not future strategy options. - Decision owner: human outside system for prospective strategy; no evidenced first-party S4 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: skills sync, user ratings, provider registry and usage statistics. - Closure path: retrospective feedback updates reusable instructions but not a prospective S4/S3 option agreement. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: auto-learning and present quota comparisons are not future-facing environmental intelligence. - Evidence: https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/session-feedback.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/agent-manager.ts - Basis: bounded negative across feedback, skills, triage, usage and monitoring. - Confidence: medium-high for selected boundary. - Caveats: external operators can plan strategy independently of 20x.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity-level legitimate ultimate-policy adjudication is implemented within local task workflows. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission checkpoints, risky task steps and user approval conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user approves/rejects ordinary work and configures application settings. - Decision owner: local human owner for task approvals; no evidenced first-party S5 policy tribunal. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: checkpoint tools, heartbeat authority gate, settings and review UI. - Closure path: individual permission may unblock a task but does not establish an identity ruling returned to organizational policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a human action approval is not by itself a governing ultimate-policy exception. - Evidence: https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/shared/system-authority.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/mcp-servers/task-management-core.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/session-feedback.ts - Basis: bounded negative across checkpoint, permissions, feedback, configuration and task completion. - Confidence: medium-high within packaged client. - Caveats: owner&#39;s real company governance could provide S5 at another recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2d9f102f34f602dcacbc9116de9fcc2e96dabeb4/assessments/20/20x.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2d9f102f34f602dcacbc9116de9fcc2e96dabeb4\">2d9f102 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T22:51:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/orka-agents/orka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orka</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/orka-agents/orka/tree/7cffe130efa74f505d66dce561ade6c18901ba6e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7cffe130efa74f505d66dce561ade6c18901ba6e\">7cffe13 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: carry out user-directed AI tasks, generate code/reviews and use first-party registered tools to produce work artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous prompts, code/test state, model/tool results, errors and task-specific context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an instantiated AI worker or coordinator chooses content-bearing tool calls and task actions based on returned observations. - Decision owner: Orka-instantiated model-driven AI Task/coordinator; external coding CLIs keep their own lower-level agent loops and are not the sole witness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Kubernetes Task CRD, pod lifecycle, credentials, workspace, provider proxy, RBAC, task timeout and durable result store. - Closure path: user/Task prompt → worker/model decision → tool or delegated action → pod/run result → subsequent model decision or returned artifact. - Boundary reachability: README&#39;s in-cluster type: ai, chat request handling and registered create/execute tools are supported paths, not only demos. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-driven selection resolves task content; fixed pod orchestration and auth do not substitute for S1 discretion. - Evidence: README; chat.go; chattoolexecutor.go; general worker. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference among sibling specialist Tasks for a configured coordinator mission, including redundant race candidates and result-dependent message handoffs. - Distinct S1 units: separately instantiated coder, reviewer, tester or peer Tasks under one coordinator/transaction, each capable of independently performing work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: redundant simultaneously running alternatives consuming capacity, stale handoffs and sibling actions on incompatible intermediate results. - Attenuating coordination relation: the coordinator may select the first useful race result and cancel other running siblings; configured send/check message and explicit dependency handoff give sibling work a shared decision route instead of independent conflicting continuation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: waitfortasks returns terminal child status/retry metadata, canceltask stops no-longer-useful work and checkmessages changes a receiver&#39;s next step. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the witness is a selected collision/duplication-interference pattern with returned control into separately executing S1s, not generic parallelism or sequential tool calls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: race/redundancy and incoherent sibling outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which running child to retain or stop and when an intermediate handoff is safe. - Decision owner: model-driven coordinator only when a coordination-enabled Agent and corresponding task patterns are configured; primitives supply the constructor but no global automatic S2 is assumed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child identity, scoped task authorization, cancellation API, durable messaging and structured terminal waits. - Closure path: concurrent child observations → coordinator judgment about winner/dependency → cancel/message/wait invocation → sibling next behavior/terminal status. - Boundary reachability: coordinated execution and control are shipped as tools, not only example YAML; a configured coordinator can call them via the first-party session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent can make the interference choice in the composed race mode; generic messenger/Task storage alone do not supply a mandatory organization-wide coordinator, hence C rather than A. - Evidence: coordination section, registered delegate, wait, cancel and messages. - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: supervise the current delegated mission, its specialist task status and correction loop, rather than all independent Kubernetes namespaces. - Whole-system current view: the configured coordinator observes the complete active mission&#39;s child-task status, rework, validation and review outcomes, not unaffiliated namespace workloads. - Current-control decision scope: current mission task assignment, retry, rework, cancellation, blocked disposition and completion readiness under the original user&#39;s authority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failed specialist deliverables, validation/review failures, blocked Tasks and incomplete current mission steps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-driven coordinator decides retry/rework/delegation/cancellation from returned child/evaluation feedback and decides when to finalize or report a blocked mission. - Decision owner: configured coordinator agent; platform timeouts and allowlists remain subordinate enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: waitfortasks terminal returns, validation and PR-CI tools, task/agent creation and update, task records, bounded retry budgets in product guidance. - Closure path: mission plan → delegated work → wait/validate/review/CI feedback → coordinator correction/delegation → revised task outputs and terminal mission disposition. - Boundary reachability: createagent coordination and optional initialPrompt initiate a coordinator and subordinate task using the supported API, and the shipped coding workflow guidance explicitly loops over failed validation/reviews before PR/CI closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordinator interprets heterogeneous current results and chooses corrections, not just fixed scheduling; no all-installation budget/policy S3 is claimed. - Evidence: chatsystemprompt.go; delegatetask.go; waitfortasks.go. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the working PR/task outcome against exact-head evidence, then make findings consequential for mission readiness and rework. - Claim being audited: a coder&#39;s PR head satisfies acceptance, safety and correctness requirements and associated validation passed. - Ordinary reporting path: coder/implementation Task produces changed files, a PR/commit and self-reported success. - Complementary access path: separately configured read-only reviewer Agent receives exact PR head SHA plus bounded changed-file/patch context; the controller rejects stale or malformed verdicts and separately verifies required validation-task provenance. - Independence boundary: dedicated reviewer Task/runtime and read-intent workspace are isolated from the implementation Task, forbidden from modifying the checkout or receiving write credentials. Reviewer model may be an external supported runtime, but Orka owns dispatch, evidence identity, ingestion and return. - Who acts on findings: RepositoryMonitor records needschanges/safety states as blocked readiness; coordinator or explicit orka:fix/orka:fix-ci repair tasks must produce a new exact-head receipt, reset stale review and re-review before readiness. Optional automerge enforces fresh review/CI and policy gates; merge never follows review text alone. - Disturbance / variety regulated: optimistic self-report, untested diffs, stale review, hidden security/correctness defects, unsupported validation claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reviewer provides an evidence-backed contrary verdict and the monitoring/workflow path can block advancement or require repair. - Decision owner: distinct reviewer Agent for substantive critique; first-party controller for trustworthy exact-head admission and current readiness state. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only workspace, signed/scoped inventory, JSON verdict schema, versioned review records, CI and separate validation provenance, bounded repair jobs. - Closure path: submitted PR → exact-head reviewer evidence → accepted verdict/blocked state → corrective agent task or human disposition → new head and re-review → readiness/merge policy. - Boundary reachability: the packaged reviewer Agent and RepositoryMonitor CRD/controller are first-party runnable paths, not a bare example prompt. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the critique is a separate model-driven reviewer and its findings are returned through first-party readiness/repair feedback; deterministic SHA/CI checks enforce validity but do not generate the semantic review. - Evidence: RepositoryMonitor, controller, review result ingestion and repair. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no distinct owner evidenced that interprets changing external prospects and selects longer-horizon adaptation of the Orka operating organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model/provider changes, future project requirements and workflow evolution were checked as plausible paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no source-backed right to convert trend/forecast/strategy intelligence into a revised ongoing mission capacity/plan, distinct from present PR repair. - Decision owner: users/administrators preconfigure Agents, providers, schedules and policies; individual coding tasks may modify repositories but that is mission S1, not system-level S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scheduled monitors, task templates, runtime-policy config, CI and dashboards. - Closure path: recurring current-state review and operational corrections are evidenced, but not future-looking intelligence → adaptation choice → changed S3 capability → later effectiveness feedback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: repeated runs, CI repair, model configuration and repository improvement do not alone prove a distinct prospective adaptation owner. - Evidence: README; chatsystemprompt.go; RepositoryMonitor guidance. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no in-boundary unresolved ultimate-identity/policy adjudication and return process is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicting goals, namespace trust and provider/tool allowances were considered but are preselected operational constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none evidenced for an independent identity-policy authority beyond fixed platform administration and task-scoped approvals. - Decision owner: cluster administrators and user/tenant policy configuration; automated admission/RBAC only enforce already chosen constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Kubernetes RBAC/admission, namespace authorization, scoped context tokens and provider/model allowlists. - Closure path: operator settings constrain later pods; no open organization identity-policy conflict → legitimate identity decision → authoritative return loop is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: project governance, role prompts, admission policy and security rules do not create a semantic S5 organ. - Evidence: README; security documentation; ToolExecutor authorization. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/10b55d8799d18dedfb18f8aaa4b1c20fd604d200/assessments/or/orka.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 10b55d8799d18dedfb18f8aaa4b1c20fd604d200\">10b55d8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T17:27:07+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hawkingrei/agenthub\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentHub</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hawkingrei/agenthub/tree/7f1f0450ebb58b3cb77b75c9669b4ad1ab8309f7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7f1f0450ebb58b3cb77b75c9669b4ad1ab8309f7\">7f1f045 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Workers deliver assigned engineering outcomes with local initiative and repair. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Implementation/test failures, new findings and scoped blockers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select local investigation/actions/repair and report evidence rather than wait for coordinator micro-approval. - Decision owner: Worker agent under the shipped worker execution role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ACP provider bridge, role-skill installation, task records, workspace tools and evidence stores. - Closure path: Task assignment/context reaches worker → agent acts → workerstatus/artifact pointers reach coordinator → accepted review or concrete change request drives continued work. - Boundary reachability: Team creation selects compiled worker prompt and role skill profile; ACP attaches mandatory managed skills before execution. External CLI is execution support, not an unexamined donor of the role’s rights. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Worker prompt explicitly gives initiative within its lane. Removing the agent leaves transport/task state without the same implementation decisions. - Evidence: skills/team/team-worker-executor.SKILL.md; crates/agenthub-team-prompts/prompts/defaultteamworkerprompt.txt; crates/agenthub-acp/src/teamroleskills.rs; src/api/teams.rs - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Prevent competing agents from simultaneously owning the same actionable mailbox topic and reconcile ownership/dependency conflicts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple workers can claim one topic or conflicting assignments; claim rejection identifies the active owner. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent chooses claim, watch/wait, release or peer/coordinator adjustment after concrete ownership feedback. - Decision owner: Participating Team agents; coordinator resolves cross-worker ambiguity when needed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SQLite topic claim/lease transaction, stable topic keys, mailbox error/status feedback and shipped mailbox discipline. - Closure path: A live conflicting claim returns ThreadClaimConflict/owner; losing agent switches to watch/wait rather than executing as a second owner; release/expiry enables later accepted work. - Boundary reachability: Both worker/coordinator ACP roles require the actor-mailbox skill. The shipped actor triage path invokes the same claim transaction and exposes its concrete conflict recovery; custom coordination code is not required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: SQL exclusion only enforces the selected claim. The mandatory agent mailbox contract supplies the behavior-changing triage response; a bare mailbox would not suffice. - Evidence: skills/team/team-actor-mailbox.SKILL.md; src/team/manager/mailboxthreads.rs; src/team/manager/mailboxerrors.rs; crates/agenthub-acp/src/teamroleskills.rs - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Source-based boundary judgment; no live model or production effectiveness claim. - Distinct S1 units: separate worker agents with assigned outcomes and local implementation discretion in the same Team. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing active ownership of one topic/task-related thread, not merely two agents exchanging messages. - Attenuating coordination relation: claim ownership plus agent watch/wait/release and explicit handoff. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: conflict response names owner and instructs loser to watch/release/wait, withholding duplicate execution until ownership permits it. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: source implements and the installed skill handles an actual exclusive-ownership collision, beyond message delivery.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Maintain cohesion of current Team work through explicit ownership, priority, accountability and intervention. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stale/duplicated tasks, blocked workers, ownership mismatch, competing priorities and unsupported completion claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose/revise canonical assignee/priority and current task commitments, park blocked work, accept/reopen based on evidence. - Decision owner: Coordinator agent in the installed Team mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Actor task create/list/update tools, task journals, execution context, mailbox evidence, role/permission gates and runtime views. - Closure path: Coordinator reads current task/member evidence → prioritizes or reassigns/changes state with journal reason → persisted canonical task/actor message changes worker’s next lane → returned evidence is reconciled again. - Boundary reachability: Compiled coordinator prompt and mandatory role skills explicitly route to task governance/lifecycle; supported actor/API tools persist chosen priorities and assignees. No adjacent contributor-management agent is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This extends beyond decomposing one prompt: the role deliberately reconciles ongoing task truth, stale duplicates, ownership drift, waiting commitments and evidence freshness. Runtime SQL/permissions execute rather than choose these decisions. - Evidence: skills/team/team-coordinator-orchestrator.SKILL.md; skills/team/team-task-governance.SKILL.md; skills/team/team-task-lifecycle.SKILL.md; src/team/manager/taskcatalog.rs; src/team/manager/taskupdates.rs; src/api/teams.rs - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Source-based boundary judgment; no live model or production effectiveness claim. - Whole-system current view: Team task set/Kanban, member identity cards and checkpoints, blockers, notes and mailbox evidence. - Current-control decision scope: owner/priority/replanning, task commitment/acceptance and block/unblock intervention across the current Team.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No additional materially independent complementary operational-audit path established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Misleading execution or management claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separately authoritative audit judgment and corrective control return. - Decision owner: Coordinator reviews ordinary worker evidence; permission reviewer decides narrower execution permission. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ACP event history, task review, permissions and developer prompt-review plugin. - Closure path: Task inreview returns requested changes to worker, but this is the normal delivery/acceptance path; no separate complementary organizational-audit mode is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Architect/reviewer naming and raw logs alone do not turn normal coordinator acceptance into S3. - Evidence: skills/team/team-task-lifecycle.SKILL.md; src/team/permissionreview/dispatcher.rs; src/team/permissionreview/selection.rs; plugins/agenthub-prompt-engineering/skills/agenthub-team-prompt-review/SKILL.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party prospective environmental adaptation loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future capability/environment change beyond current task learning. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Develop/admit prospective organizational capability options from external distinctions. - Decision owner: Agents retain project lessons and may patch their own profile; user controls broader product evolution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Workspace memory, profilepatchproposal, timed follow-ups and runtime recovery spine. - Closure path: Selected notes/profile changes inform later tasks, without an established external/future option-to-capability regulatory conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Self-profile drift repair and durable lessons do not alone establish S4; planned Mem/activation design cannot supply current closure. - Evidence: docs/features/team-workspace-memory-contract.md; src/api/teams.rs; skills/team/team-worker-executor.SKILL.md; README.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No legitimate ultimate-policy/identity adjudication loop at Team recursion established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Purpose/ultimate-policy tension, beyond member identity metadata and execution risk. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Return an authoritative ultimate-purpose/policy decision into subsequent Team operation. - Decision owner: User configures Team; agents own only scoped self-profile proposals and operational commitments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent cards, role skill restrictions, profile patches and permission approvals. - Closure path: Description/prompt changes update local role metadata; ACP approvals permit scoped commands. Neither is an evidenced ultimate-policy decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The word identity in Agent Cards does not make role-metadata editing S5. - Evidence: src/api/teams.rs; skills/team/TEAMAGENTS.md; skills/team/team-task-governance.SKILL.md; src/team/permissionreview/dispatcher.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/16805b7f353f8cb58b1aafb55c1c3f20ef01076a/assessments/ag/agenthub.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 16805b7f353f8cb58b1aafb55c1c3f20ef01076a\">16805b7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T22:11:16+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Chorus-AIDLC/Chorus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Chorus</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Chorus-AIDLC/Chorus/tree/148b7928b99773f7fbdf61080940bd6995b06934\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 148b7928b99773f7fbdf61080940bd6995b06934\">148b792 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously implement bounded Chorus tasks and produce software changes/evidence under the task&#39;s acceptance criteria and project context. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository state, implementation choices, failures, tests, project constraints and task-specific ambiguity encountered while completing assigned work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive coding/tool/debugging actions needed to satisfy an assigned task and decide when work is ready to submit for verification. - Decision owner: the autonomous task worker agent launched under the shipped Chorus develop/worker protocol. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task/proposal state, acceptance criteria, permissions, session/daemon dispatch, agent integration adapters, Chorus MCP tools and persisted work reports. - Closure path: orchestrator dispatches an unblocked task → worker reads Chorus context and executes autonomously in the target repository → worker reports work/self-check and submits for verification → resulting state/evidence returns to Chorus for audit/control. - Boundary reachability: Chorus ships the worker/develop skill contracts and supported adapters/daemon paths that launch autonomous coding-agent sessions as the standard operating surface; the external coding harness supplies execution capabilities but the Chorus project/task contract and return path are first-party. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the task contract constrains scope, while the running worker agent chooses the substantive implementation sequence and tool actions rather than Chorus deterministically prescribing them. - Evidence: README.md, $yolo task execution. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: S1 autonomy is credited to the running task-agent role reachable through Chorus&#39;s first-party integration surfaces, not to deterministic task-state machinery or to undocumented behavior of any particular external coding harness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material S2-specific regulation of a demonstrated interference/oscillation among distinct S1 workers is established at the declared project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as an S2 disturbance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as S2. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task DAG dependencies, unblocked-task queries, dependency-ordered waves, project/task state, comments and worker isolation organize and sequence work but do not by themselves establish an inter-S1 conflict/oscillation witness. - Closure path: dependency completion changes which tasks become runnable, but this is task sequencing/current control rather than evidence of a separate S2 attenuation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: multiple workers and a DAG are insufficient under the Profile; the reviewed evidence does not identify a concrete recurring interference between S1 units and a distinct coordination relation targeted at attenuating that interference. - Evidence: $yolo wave execution, README.md. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: dependency ordering can prevent invalid execution order, but no separate S2-specific interference/attenuation relation is evidenced strongly enough to publish a positive state.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a project-wide current operational view and decide which commitments start, wait, reopen, advance or escalate. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete dependencies, failed or missing worker submissions, task-review failures, proposal-review failures, stuck work, exhausted review rounds and changing readiness across the task DAG. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next dependency-ready task wave, dispatch workers, decide how to handle non-ready/failed results, reopen failed tasks, advance verified tasks and stop/escalate when the project cannot progress. - Decision owner: the model-driven orchestrating agent executing the first-party $yolo workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent project/proposal/task state, chorusgetunblockedtasks, status transitions, acceptance criteria, admin transition APIs, reviewer comments, permissions and daemon/session lifecycle. - Closure path: orchestrator reads current project/task/review state → chooses wave/advance/reopen/escalate action → Chorus state/API transitions and worker dispatch implement it → changed task states alter subsequent readiness and execution → refreshed project state returns to the orchestrator. - Boundary reachability: $yolo is a shipped first-party Chorus skill and explicitly provides a complete autonomous mode using Chorus MCP/project state; its current-control decisions are not borrowed from repository CI or human project administration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-state and dependency code enforce constraints, but the orchestrating agent chooses the current portfolio action, handles exceptional results and decides whether to continue, rework or escalate. - Whole-system current view: project, proposal, task DAG, unblocked tasks, task statuses, review comments/verdicts and completion state across the Chorus project. - Current-control decision scope: current worker commitments, wave dispatch, rework/reopen, progression after review, and escalation when progress conditions cannot be satisfied. - Evidence: plugins/chorus/skills/yolo/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: server-side validation, permission checks and dependency computation are enforcement/support. S3 credit comes from the orchestrating agent&#39;s evidenced project-wide current-control choices.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge proposal and implementation completion claims through separate read-only reviewer agents and return blocking findings into corrective project operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missing requirements, weak acceptance criteria, dependency/design errors, implementation drift, circular or inadequate self-tests, build/test failure and developer self-verification blind spots. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect evidence independently, classify findings as blocking/non-blocking and issue the current round&#39;s VERDICT that determines whether the orchestrator advances or returns the work for correction. - Decision owner: the separate proposal-reviewer or task-reviewer agent for the corresponding audit stage. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only reviewer permissions, proposal/task/Idea/comment APIs, filesystem/test/build inspection, structured verdict format, persisted comments, review-round limits and admin state-transition APIs. - Closure path: producer submits proposal/task → distinct reviewer gathers complementary evidence and posts current-round verdict → orchestrator reads that verdict → FAIL causes reject/revise/resubmit or reopen/rework → the corrected artifact is reviewed again before advancement. - Boundary reachability: both reviewer definitions and the orchestration that invokes/consumes them are shipped first-party Chorus plugin surfaces in the standard autonomous workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reviewers make substantive adversarial judgments from separate evidence and are prevented from editing the work under review; deterministic state transitions only enforce the downstream response selected from the verdict. - Claim being audited: proposal completeness/alignment before implementation and task implementation/acceptance correctness before verification/completion. - Ordinary reporting path: PM/developer agents create proposal drafts or implementation, self-check/report work and submit it through ordinary Chorus state transitions. - Complementary access path: reviewer agents separately fetch originating Idea/proposal/task/AC/comments and inspect project files, diffs, tests/builds or proposal dependency coverage under read-only constraints. - Independence boundary: reviewer roles are separate spawned agents with explicit read-only restrictions and distinct reviewer contracts; they do not author the proposal/code they evaluate during the review pass. - Who acts on findings: the $yolo orchestrator reads the current verdict and rejects/reopens or approves/verifies accordingly, causing the affected planning/development agents to perform subsequent correction. - Evidence: proposal reviewer, task reviewer, $yolo review loops. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: reviewer verdict strings are not credited merely because they are called review; the positive state rests on separate evidence access, read-only independence and an explicit findings-to-rework closure.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then adaptation loop is established at the Chorus-project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S4 scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project search, Idea elaboration, proposal generation, repository/context inspection, persistent reports and reviewer feedback improve current project execution but do not establish prospective environment sensing that changes the harness/project&#39;s future capability program. - Closure path: current-project information feeds current planning and rework; no separate future-facing adaptation selection/return loop was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning future tasks inside the current product request and learning from current review are not sufficient to map S4 without an environment-facing prospective adaptation loop. - Evidence: $yolo planning/review workflow, README.md. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Chorus can be extended and its agents can research externally, but generic agent capability or project planning does not establish S4 ownership for the harness at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the Chorus-project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S5 scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as identity/ultimate policy. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user prompt/Idea intent, API-key permissions, admin approval/verification rights, project settings and review-round limits constrain operation but are operational/task policy rather than an evidenced S5 identity loop. - Closure path: permissions and approvals affect allowed current actions, but no disputed identity/ultimate-policy matter is routed to an authoritative S5 actor and returned as governing identity policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a user origin, admin permission or approval gate is not automatically S5; the reviewed project boundary does not expose an identity-level policy negotiation/closure path. - Evidence: README.md, $yolo prerequisite/admin flow. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: humans can operate/administer Chorus and configure permissions, but those facts alone do not establish a parent-governed S5 function under Methodology 0.3.5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/60fa7f6cbad5d00ad76aac4e43ee85735aaa106f/assessments/chorus.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 60fa7f6cbad5d00ad76aac4e43ee85735aaa106f\">60fa7f6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-16T21:07:46+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Traves-Theberge/Fetch\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Fetch</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-16\">2026-11-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Traves-Theberge/Fetch/tree/efb6e0b4a8b72e436929b44a355a29d02e72a221\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision efb6e0b4a8b72e436929b44a355a29d02e72a221\">efb6e0b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce workspace, research and delegated coding outcomes on user requests. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain work state, varied user requests, and tool/provider responses. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose and execute tools, adapt after each result and complete or delegate the task. - Decision owner: The Fetch model-backed agent in the channel session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bridge, provider transport, session loop, tool registry, Kennel and optional harness drivers. - Closure path: Tool execution results are persisted into the active session and fed into later model decisions before returning replies or starting a task. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model selects workspace and task actions. The registry and adapter implement selected calls. - Evidence: fetch-app/src/handler/index.ts, fetch-app/src/agent/core.ts, fetch-app/src/agent/core.ts, fetch-app/src/tools/task.ts - Basis: structural - Confidence: High for shipped source reachability. - Caveats: Delegated harness internals are external. The Fetch lead&#39;s own model/tool loop supplies S1. - Boundary reachability: The bundled bridge invokes the shipped processMessage loop and task tools in the supported channel mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Prevent independently active chat sessions from taking incompatible concurrent commitments on the bridge&#39;s shared delegated slot. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two authorized sessions can issue coding requests while the same process-wide task slot is occupied. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Act on active-slot and task status feedback to defer a competing start or coordinate a changed commitment. - Decision owner: Fetch models in the calling sessions. The process-wide task manager enforces the single-slot rule. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session-scoped model runs, singleton task manager, tracked task IDs and busy error with existing-task context. - Closure path: A competing start is rejected with the current task ID and a path for follow-up control. The requesting model receives the refusal and can defer or route a change, and a terminal release allows a later start. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The slot check is deterministic. The calling model owns its subsequent choice of actions or feedback based on the identified competing commitment. - Evidence: fetch-app/src/handler/index.ts, fetch-app/src/session/manager.ts, fetch-app/src/task/manager.ts, fetch-app/src/task/manager.ts, fetch-app/src/tools/task.ts, fetch-app/src/agent/core.ts - Basis: structural - Confidence: Moderate for the bounded allocation disturbance. - Caveats: The witness concerns contention for the singleton delegated slot. It supplies no general multi-task file-level arbitration. - Boundary reachability: Independent authorized channel sessions converge on the same singleton through the bundled taskcreate tool. - Distinct S1 units: Independently running model-backed channel sessions handling different user requests. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Both can try to submit a delegated job when only one active job can use the shared slot. - Attenuating coordination relation: Singleton task admission and the corrective busy response naming the conflicting active job. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The caller receives the conflict as a tool result, so its next action can defer or seek a justified changed commitment rather than launching another job. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: These calls meet on a concrete exclusive shared capacity. The busy result changes the requesting unit&#39;s subsequent action.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate an already active bridge-wide delegated commitment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An occupied slot may be blocked or the active work may need to change direction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect the current commitment and choose whether and what to cancel or replace in the currently authorized task scope. - Decision owner: The Fetch model in the tool-led path, and the authorized channel operator in the distinct deterministic command path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task status/index, cancel tool, active process termination and persisted task transitions. - Closure path: Cancellation targets the running process when present and clears the current-task pointer, then returns outcome state. Subsequent tool work can start on the released capacity. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model or operator selects the discretionary intervention. The manager and the adapter apply it. - Evidence: fetch-app/src/agent/response-policy.ts, fetch-app/src/tools/task.ts, fetch-app/src/tools/task.ts, fetch-app/src/task/manager.ts, fetch-app/src/handler/index.ts, fetch-app/src/commands/task.ts - Basis: structural - Confidence: Moderate to high for current one-slot control. - Caveats: An explicit user order to cancel is parent-owned. The base witness is model choice of intervention from returned current-task state within its task authority. - Boundary reachability: The first-party task tool group exposes status and cancellation. The direct /stop route reaches the same underlying commitment without a model turn. - Whole-system current view: The entire currently admitted delegated workload is one active task. The most recent status/progress can be retrieved by default active-task lookup and operator status. - Current-control decision scope: Cancel or replace the active bridge-wide delegated commitment and free the shared execution slot.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No shipped audit path independently samples and adjudicates delegated work as a control loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Delegated runner summaries may differ from actual working-tree or application outcome. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The installed task result path records the runner outcome. Application and browser checks are exposed separately as caller-selected tests. - Decision owner: Task adapter and orchestrator own ordinary operational checks. No independent auditor with corrective authority is evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task result mapping, status/notifications, shipped application and browser test tools, workflow automation. - Closure path: Normal completion is recorded from the harness result. Caller-defined workflows can inspect artifacts but the reviewed distribution does not bind them as independent task audit with a corrective return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Different containers or a test tool do not establish independent audit judgment on their own. - Evidence: fetch-app/src/task/integration.ts, fetch-app/src/skills/builtin/task-orchestration/SKILL.md, fetch-app/src/skills/builtin/workflow-automation/SKILL.md, fetch-app/src/tools/workflow.ts - Basis: structural - Confidence: Moderate. - Caveats: A deployment can explicitly arrange independent validation using these tools, but no supplied audit-specific ownership loop was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective environmental adaptation loop was established in the reviewed operating surface. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Project differences, saved experience and external software versions vary over time. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Retain contextual notes or apply a prescribed release according to an operator-selected action. No first-party adaptive judgment from environmental observations was established. - Decision owner: The operator owns deployment choices. Note extraction and release application are automatic support. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session memory and summaries, workflow persistence, manifest updates and host manager. - Closure path: Persisted notes and workflows influence subsequent tasks. The host updater installs the selected channel&#39;s release. These paths do not develop and choose a case-specific future operating capability from the environment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Applying released code or replaying a saved routine is supplied maintenance and operation, with no independent prospective capability decision established. - Evidence: fetch-app/src/agent/core.ts, fetch-app/src/session/manager.ts, fetch-app/src/skills/builtin/workflow-automation/SKILL.md, manager/main.go, manager/main.go, manager/main.go, scripts/fetch-cli.sh - Basis: structural - Confidence: Moderate at the deployed bridge boundary. - Caveats: A separately configured research or policy workflow could implement a closed S4 process.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Maintain the deployment&#39;s ultimate policy for which principals may direct Fetch through WhatsApp. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Whether an additional requester should permanently be admitted or an existing requester should lose access. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide an add/revoke issue and return the authoritative admission decision into the bridge policy. - Decision owner: The configured deployment owner, who alone can use the channel trust-control command. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Sender identity gate, persisted whitelist and bridged command parser. - Closure path: Owner decision is written to the shared whitelist backing subsequent request admission. Future requests from the affected principal are accepted or refused according to the returned decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model has no role in deciding these grants. The direct owner command and subsequent bridge gate supply the evidenced policy loop. - Evidence: fetch-app/src/commands/parser.ts, fetch-app/src/commands/trust.ts, fetch-app/src/security/whitelist.ts, fetch-app/src/security/gate.ts, fetch-app/src/bridge/client.ts - Basis: structural - Confidence: High for the source-backed WhatsApp admission policy route. - Caveats: The grant scope is who may issue requests through the declared WhatsApp bridge. Environment-supplied membership and other channel authorization remain separate. - Boundary reachability: The standard WhatsApp ingress accepts owner-authorized commands and routes /trust to the shared whitelist consulted on later ingress. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Decide who is allowed to direct this deployment, beyond authorizing a particular tool call or task. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: The deployment owner identified by configuration and checked before whitelist change. - Return-to-operation path: Owner issues add/revoke, whitelist state persists, and later bridge ingress applies that durable result.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f1c0cc3697e767ad88adeb2b2bfbe844d2fed6b5/assessments/fe/fetch.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f1c0cc3697e767ad88adeb2b2bfbe844d2fed6b5\">f1c0cc3 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T21:13:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sd0xdev/sd0x-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">sd0x-dev-flow</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sd0xdev/sd0x-harness/tree/04e8a5e3f5d9c482937c2ad2170fab5fbd02b630\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 04e8a5e3f5d9c482937c2ad2170fab5fbd02b630\">04e8a5e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement and verify a requested software change or investigation in a user project through model-directed project work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar project context, failing tests, faulty edits, changed requirements and review findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose inspections, edits, tests, delegated specialist assistance and repairs in response to evidence. - Decision owner: model-driven Claude Code engineering actor using the installed first-party project rules and specialist skills; the external Claude host executes tool calls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plugin rules, installed project setup, hooks, review-state digest, git guards and verification scripts. - Closure path: user task → actor work and tools → test/review evidence or failure → actor correction/re-verification → project changes and report. - Boundary reachability: the documented full Claude plugin mode installs skills/rules/hooks and the session imports them; unlike skills-only mode, the full mode supplies the completion and review path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the actor selects engineering actions, whereas scripts record and gate outcomes without choosing code changes; external host internals are not credited as first-party decisions. - Evidence: installation and ownership model, active session rules, project setup and installed surfaces, hook wiring. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: source demonstrates a supported installed mode, not deployment frequency or measured task success; first-party credit is for the composed plugin mode, not the external host&#39;s general autonomous capabilities.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct inter-S1 interference regulation is established for the assessed standard coding session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: read-only fanout and specialist handoffs are present, but no concrete conflicting changes between autonomous S1 work cells are regulated by a specified returned coordination decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: not established; planner/executor role dispatch and report ordering do not themselves decide collision attenuation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: report-only workflow planning, bounded read-only fanout and sequential specialist skill calls. - Closure path: workflow reports return to the caller; no evidenced change to later competing S1 behaviour from conflict regulation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-driven orchestration of independent read-only findings alone is not S2. - Evidence: read-only orchestrate contract, single reviewer default, specialist definition. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: other user-composed multi-agent modes are possible but are not credited without a shipped interference-specific return loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-system current-control relation is established at the configured project-session boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risk severity, code/doc dirtiness and review failures are constrained by local quality gates, not shown as current resource bargaining across operational units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: risk tier and gate obligations operate on task/change evidence without an evidenced whole-system allocation or corrective control decision. - Decision owner: model and user choose execution/review details within static policy; digest-state scripts enforce/report. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Anchor Register, tier selection, review slots, hook reminders, scoped guards and human exits. - Closure path: review failures cause local work/review retries; no independently evidenced S3 current-control feedback loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent-owned local repair and deterministic completion invariant do not themselves establish S3. - Evidence: review tiers and loop, policy tiers and ownership, digest-bound state, nonblocking hook. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: risk-scaled verification is important but cannot substitute for a whole-system current view.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge an implementer&#39;s completion/correctness claim by inspecting actual changed project artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a producer may falsely declare completion or miss faults not visible in its own summary/checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: give a separate severity-scoped audit verdict from raw code/diff access and determine whether corrective work is owed. - Decision owner: the separately dispatched Codex reviewer model makes the audit judgment; the producing Claude actor owns subsequent repairs; no digest parser is credited as auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only codex exec transport, frozen changed-file metadata, review-state verdict slot, risk thresholds and fallback validation. - Closure path: after code changes, /codex-review-fast → independent reviewer source investigation → Ready/Blocked findings → producing actor fixes blocking issues → re-review → fresh pass records a digest-bound slot required before completion. - Boundary reachability: the full plugin installs the review skill, transport and model-facing auto-loop obligations, and hooks remind after edits; the review is a documented first-party supported optional tool path, not a CI-only or developer-dogfood audit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a different model agent reads the actual diff/source and decides findings; transport, severity parser and receipt record do not substitute for its judgment. - Claim being audited: project change is correct enough under the configured risk tier to be complete. - Ordinary reporting path: producer&#39;s change summary, tests and its own verification. - Complementary access path: separate Codex sandbox source/diff inspection and independent review prompt; fallback reviewers are controlled by recorded transport failure semantics. - Independence boundary: read-only external Codex session receives frozen task and changed-file metadata but reads source itself; it is not the producer&#39;s self-verification. Both hosted tools remain outside first-party implementation ownership. - Who acts on findings: producing Claude actor performs scoped fixes and re-dispatches; human is reserved for explicitly enumerated exits. - Evidence: independent source review and fix loop, severity/scope gate, separate reviewer transport, repeat-until-current-pass contract, digest-bound return. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: evidence is shipped protocol/code, not a captured successful live independent audit; review may be blocked by missing external Codex credentials or human exits in particular installations.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: adapt subsequent project-engineering guidance from recurring external/user corrections. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated user-observed failures whose recurrence is not prevented by current rules. - Decisive decision or feedback right: distinguish a reusable error pattern and, after repetition, update project-local operational guidance for future sessions. - Decision owner: the model-driven engineering actor following the first-party self-improvement rule; the user can originate corrections and decide version-control inclusion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: namespaced lesson log, repeated-pattern criterion, rule-file update and normal doc review. - Closure path: user correction on a recurrent failure → actor writes a structured lesson with prevention signal → pattern reaches promotion criterion → actor promotes guidance to project rules → future sessions read updated rules and act differently. - Boundary reachability: the published self-improvement rule is installed via standard full-plugin project setup and imported by session guidance; the namespaced lesson file is provisioned on first occurrence and later local rules are loaded. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model must recognize the pattern, select the prevention and write/publish the operational rule; file persistence alone is not the adaptation judgment. - External distinction: user correction provides external feedback about unmet project expectations, not solely internal planning. - Future / prospective distinction: lesson/rule promotion addresses future recurrence across tasks and sessions. - Adaptation option generated: prevention rule and detection signal reusable for later work. - Path back into current capability / S3: project-installed rules govern later Claude sessions, closing adaptation into operational capability; no distinct S3 is claimed. - Evidence: correction-to-rule procedure, rule installation, project rule imports. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: contingent on actual recurring correction and session-level rule promotion; source does not establish measured improvement or autonomous change to the plugin&#39;s published universal anchors.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision-and-return loop is established in the supported project engineering session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: local configuration, security anchors, scope requests and approval/merge risks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no in-boundary identity-level decision is returned through an established S5 channel. - Decision owner: developer and user set framework identity and non-negotiable rules; model may deviate from defaults only inside those constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fixed Anchor Register, project overrides, user confirmation and protected git/review gates. - Closure path: local tasks use preselected rules; no evidenced policy-level proposal → legitimate parent judgment → returned change to the session&#39;s identity/ultimate policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static constraint hierarchy and user approval of ordinary actions do not create S5 closure. - Evidence: Anchor/Default boundaries, fixed security policy, scope and developer role. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: project maintainers can change the published plugin policy in a different recursion, which is explicitly excluded here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d6959de776c5df5b41ae7fb77c94cfd9bd6236ee/assessments/sd/sd0x-dev-flow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d6959de776c5df5b41ae7fb77c94cfd9bd6236ee\">d6959de &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T21:37:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/coleam00/your-claude-engineer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Your Claude Engineer</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/coleam00/your-claude-engineer/tree/e7c064ac915cfeb29496c558795efa125a121863\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e7c064ac915cfeb29496c558795efa125a121863\">e7c064a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement a selected app feature through model-selected coding actions, browser tests and correction from tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete feature requirements, changing code context, failing browser flows, runtime errors and interrupted long-running model sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: coding model chooses concrete edits, commands, browser inspections and repairs from the orchestrator&#39;s issue context and observed tool outcomes. - Decision owner: model-backed coding subagent executed through Claude Agent SDK; the first-party harness supplies role/prompt/tool configuration and continuity, not the SDK model&#39;s entire general capability. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generated project directory, first-party subagent definitions, Arcade tools, sandbox, bash pretool hook and retry/session boundaries. - Closure path: app specification and Linear issue → orchestrator delegates detailed work → coding model inspects/edits/tests → Playwright or tool result → fix and verification → implementation evidence returned to orchestrator/Linear. - Boundary reachability: autonomousagentdemo.py invokes the first-party run loop; client.py wires AGENTDEFINITIONS and built-in/Playwright tools into the SDK client; coding prompt directs real code/test actions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: implementation and repair actions depend on model interpretation of evidence; the shell allowlist, iteration delay and state markers cannot make those decisions alone. - Evidence: entry/session loop, SDK client and tool wiring, specialized coding definition, coding prompt. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a live provider/Arcade/Playwright session was not run; SDK authentication and third-party success remain outside the evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct mechanism is shown for attenuating a concrete disturbance between independently viable, competing operational S1 coding units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Linear, coding, GitHub and Slack agents exchange messages and roles, but no competing source-changing S1 claims or resource-contention disturbance is evidenced in the default sequential one-issue flow. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific allocation, negotiated conflict-resolution or revised reciprocal S1 commitments. - Decision owner: none shown; orchestrator transfers context and orders specialized tasks, while SDK Task dispatch executes bounded child work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role separation, Linear statuses, one-issue sequencing, local generated-project git and task handoff summaries. - Closure path: orchestrator orders a single implementation issue and passes results to Linear/GitHub/Slack; this is cross-role task routing, not a source-backed interference-damping return between contending operational cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model-chosen next issue belongs to whole-current S3 at this recursion; it does not itself close S2 competition among independent parallel work units. - Evidence: explicit one-issue-at-a-time rule and context transfer, sequential feature workflow, role definitions. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: external SDK may support parallel Task calls, but this source does not demonstrate a distinct concrete S2 attenuation path for the shipped project workflow.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current whole project backlog, work priority and resumed progress across many feature issues/sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stale issue status, wrong next-work choice, incomplete previously Done features, session context resets and missing acceptance evidence. - Whole-system current view: the model orchestrator requests Linear META context, counts across Done/In Progress/Todo and full details of the next issue, and reads the generated project&#39;s current state before deciding its continuation. - Current-control decision scope: model selects whether to stop and repair regression or proceed to the next feature, delegates the issue and decides when to mark it Done based on required browser evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which pending portfolio issue should be worked next and overturn current progress when verification fails; issue transitions/continuation return to Linear and next fresh session. - Decision owner: first-party prompted model orchestrator with live Linear agent and coding-agent feedback; neither static CLI loop nor Linear SaaS is credited with the model&#39;s project-wide decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: META issue, .linearproject.json continuation marker, project progress summary, subagent tools, status counts, completion signal. - Closure path: fresh session reads project/Linear whole-current status → orchestrator asks for retest → if failed directs correction, else chooses next issue → dispatches code and evidence → updates Linear Done/next state → next session refreshes the picture. - Boundary reachability: the active client loads orchestratorprompt.md and the iteration loop recreates a client per session, so this is not an unused design diagram. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the prompt gives the model discretion to choose/reprioritize actual current work and act on regression findings; the deterministic loop only reopens sessions and recognizes the completion token. - Evidence: orchestrator continuation and priority/verification steps, whole-project completion decision, fresh-session state and rerun, live prompt registration. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: the whole here is the one generated app&#39;s issue portfolio, not all of an enterprise&#39;s projects; the Linear account&#39;s actual data/permissions were not accessed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: test prior completed-feature claims against actual running UI/interaction evidence before the S3 orchestrator takes new work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Linear may say Done while a previously working feature has regressed; summary or screenshot receipts can be stale or misleading. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model-driven coding subagent runs a fresh browser verification for 1–2 completed features and can return FAIL plus screenshots; orchestrator must suspend new work and request regression fixing. - Decision owner: separately invoked coding verifier model for direct Playwright observations; orchestrator owns current feature plan and the fix order. This is the same coding role as producers, but a distinct subsequent Task invocation, not a third-party auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mandatory pre-work verification gate, browser navigation/click/snapshot/screenshot tools, structured PASS/FAIL report, no-Done-without-screenshot rule. - Closure path: prior Linear Done claim → new continuation requests browser retest → independent current app UI observations → FAIL evidence → orchestrator orders fix and re-test before new task → updated issue/current project status. - Boundary reachability: orchestrator prompt makes retest mandatory on every continuation/new feature; coding subagent prompt defines a dedicated Verification Test task; Playwright tools are registered in the normal SDK client. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model verifier decides whether the live UI corroborates the prior completion account; neither the prior producer&#39;s summary nor a deterministic status parser supplies this judgment. - Claim being audited: previously marked-Done project features still work and are fit to leave closed. - Ordinary reporting path: Linear Done status, prior implementation/test summary and saved project/meta issue context. - Complementary access path: fresh coding verifier goes to running app via Playwright, exercises 1–2 completed features, observes screenshots/browser state and reports PASS/FAIL. - Independence boundary: verifier is a fresh delegated Task/subagent context relative to prior iteration and S3 issue status, but reuses the &#39;coding&#39; role/provider and is not organizationally independent; that narrower evidence-based independence is the claim credited. - Who acts on findings: S3 orchestrator blocks next feature and asks coding agent to repair a reproduced regression; verification repeats before onward progress. - Evidence: mandatory verification and remedial gate, continuation&#39;s direct retest and failure return, coding agent browser verification protocol, tool registry. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: prompts specify behavior; no captured live regression was reproduced in this review. The same coding agent role is reused, so independence is bounded to fresh contextual sensing versus S3/earlier Done account, not external independent peer review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped organizational loop selects durable prospective changes to the harness&#39;s future capabilities from external environmental intelligence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user-specified app goals and task regressions are operations/current control, not an ongoing externally informed future adaptation mandate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none beyond issue-by-issue project implementation and user-edited prompts/spec/config. - Decision owner: user supplies app specification and permission settings; orchestrator chooses present feature priority but does not autonomously revise the harness&#39;s strategic operating design. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: appspec.txt, developer-editable prompt files, Linear backlog, META continuation memory and model configuration flags. - Closure path: spec/backlog → present coding and feature selection; no external change signal → strategic option → selected lasting future operating-capability shift is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: long-running context and future work items are ordinary S1/S3 continuity, not independent S4 environmental search/adaptation. - External distinction: third-party project requirements and Linear status are task inputs, not autonomous environmental foresight. - Future / prospective distinction: an unfinished backlog is a plan for task execution, not adaptation of the executing organization&#39;s future structure. - Evidence: project specification and prompt customization, current issue workflow, session continuity. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a user can change the prompts or request a different application; that is external reconfiguration, not a built-in strategic return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party autonomous ultimate-policy or legitimate organizational identity owner closes an S5 constitutional return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool permission, command safety, OAuth authorization and product scope are externally pre-set restrictions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none for determining the project&#39;s ultimate legitimate purpose or amendments to binding identity/policy. - Decision owner: project owner, external credentials administrator and fixed script/security policies govern these matters. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claude SDK sandbox, file tool allowlist, bash PreToolUse hook, Arcade OAuth, fixed appspec and allowed-command list. - Closure path: configuration constrains tool execution; no first-party deliberation over constitutional goals and downward reauthorization is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: enforcement of static user/configuration rights is not policy-identity formation. - Evidence: security and permission construction, SDK tool/hook invocation, first-party bash guard. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high within reviewed boundary. - Caveats: external user/organization policy is excluded from this system in focus.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/21315010a2fe15de7f6fd1c8c001cbedcbb865f7/assessments/yo/your-claude-engineer.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 21315010a2fe15de7f6fd1c8c001cbedcbb865f7\">2131501 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T18:54:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/affaan-m/ECC\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Everything Claude Code (ECC)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-21\">2026-09-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/affaan-m/ECC/tree/2b6e839771e53096d8451a213d40dc64ec8acac0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2b6e839771e53096d8451a213d40dc64ec8acac0\">2b6e839 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute assigned project work inside ECC-managed model-driven sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task ambiguity, repository state, tool/test observations, failures, intermediate artifacts and uncertainty about the next substantive action. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose implementation/tool actions and iterate from observations until the assigned task completes or terminates. - Decision owner: the model-driven agent session launched/managed by ECC. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ECC2 lifecycle/store, worktrees, process launch, session identity, output capture, stop/resume and NanoClaw persistence where used. - Closure path: assigned task → ECC-managed model session → model-selected actions → repository/tool observations → further model actions → session result/state. - Boundary reachability: ECC2 start and the session manager directly launch supported model-driven agent sessions; NanoClaw is also shipped first-party. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ECC lifecycle code manages the process but does not choose its substantive next coding/tool action. - Evidence: ecc2/src/main.rs, ecc2/src/session/manager.rs, scripts/claw.js. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the launched host harness retains its own internal tool-loop implementation; its S2–S5 semantics are not imported.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concrete collision risk among parallel agent S1 units working on overlapping/coupled repository state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: same-file/line overlap and dependency/tree coupling that can make concurrent changes interfere. - Decisive decision or feedback right: compute right-of-way and an S2-specific hold/steer advisory; a return consumer must still apply that maneuver to the affected S1. - Decision owner: no autonomous owner is closed over the full loop; current right-of-way is deterministic and the agent-return actor is absent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: working-set extraction, dependency graph, overlap/dependency/tree risk channels, priority calculation, advisory feed and worktree isolation. - Closure path: parallel worksets → collision-risk calculation → hold/steer advisory → constructor gap at the return leg into S1 behavior. - Boundary reachability: the scanner and advisory/event contract are shipped first-party paths specifically designed around inter-agent collision; the missing TCAS hook prevents A. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic right-of-way calculation is not autonomous organizational ownership; ECC exposes the S2-specific path but not its autonomous closure. - Evidence: docs/design/agent-proximity.md, docs/control-plane/VIEW-CONTRACT.md, docs/control-plane/TCAS-HOOK.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: TCAS-HOOK.md explicitly marks the return hook design-only; older design prose must not be read as proof that steering is already wired. - Distinct S1 units: separate ECC-managed model-driven sessions/worktrees acting concurrently on the same project. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping edits and dependency-coupled changes create concrete collision/merge risk between those sessions. - Attenuating coordination relation: ECC proximity computes pairwise risk, priority/right-of-way and a hold/steer maneuver advisory. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: ECC exposes the action-bearing advisory as the S2-specific return primitive, but the consumer that applies it before an edit remains to be composed; this is the constructor gap. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the relation exists specifically to reduce measured cross-agent code collision, with explicit pairwise risk and maneuver semantics rather than merely moving messages or tasks.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: regulate current team/session commitments, allocation, backlog pressure, intervention and worktree integration across an ECC-managed project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing active/stopped sessions, unread handoffs, idle/saturated delegates, backlog pressure, worktree readiness and current need to add/reuse/rebalance/stop capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose delegation/assignment/rebalance/stop/resume/merge actions from current team state. - Decision owner: constructor mode — autonomous owner must be composed; parent mode — the human operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session/message store, dashboard/team/status, delegate-selection scoring, backlog counts, worktree state and daemon/process lifecycle. - Closure path: current team/backlog/worktree view → operator or composed controller chooses intervention → ECC executes it → commitments/state change → refreshed view reflects the result. - Boundary reachability: ECC2 ships Delegate/Assign/AutoDispatch/CoordinateBacklog/Rebalance/Stop/Merge surfaces and current team/status views. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic assignment/maintenance can execute policy but does not constitute a model-owned whole-team discretionary judgment; the operator path is separately closed. - Evidence: ecc2/src/main.rs, ecc2/src/session/manager.rs, skills/team-agent-orchestration/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ECC2 is explicitly alpha; roadmap-only autonomous orchestration is not credited. - Whole-system current view: dashboard/session/team/coordination-status surfaces expose active sessions, delegate relationships, backlog/current state and worktree information at the project recursion. - Current-control decision scope: delegation/reuse/spawn, backlog redistribution, stop/resume, team intervention and worktree merge/integration.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently audit a candidate using complementary reviewer actors and return defects into corrective production work before shipping. - Disturbance / variety regulated: semantic defects, hallucinations, incompleteness, compliance violations and technical errors the producer may miss. - Decisive decision or feedback right: two isolated reviewers independently issue structured PASS/FAIL judgments; any failure blocks shipping and supplies issues to a fix cycle followed by fresh reviewers. - Decision owner: the two independent reviewer agents for audit judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: identical rubric/input contract, context isolation, structured verdicts, deterministic both-pass gate, bounded iterations and escalation. - Closure path: candidate → independent reviewers → findings → both-pass gate or fix issue set → revised candidate → fresh reviewers → ship or bounded escalation. - Boundary reachability: Santa Method is a shipped ECC skill intended for supported hosts with subagent capability and specifies the full reviewer/correction topology. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the semantic audit judgments come from separate model actors; deterministic code only aggregates their verdicts. - Evidence: skills/santa-method/SKILL.md, commands/santa-loop.md. - Basis: explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: strongest independence requires a supported host&#39;s separate subagent contexts; the documented inline fallback is weaker and is not the basis for A. - Claim being audited: the produced candidate&#39;s correctness/completeness/compliance against the original specification and rubric. - Ordinary reporting path: the producing agent&#39;s own output and deterministic build/test checks remain the normal production evidence. - Complementary access path: two fresh reviewer agents independently receive the same original specification, candidate and rubric outside the producer&#39;s context. - Independence boundary: reviewers are separate agent contexts/processes and do not share each other&#39;s assessments; fresh reviewer instances are required after fixes. - Who acts on findings: a fix/production agent receives the merged critical issues, revises the candidate and submits it to fresh review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: turn operational experience and corrections into persistent learned behaviors that alter later ECC-hosted sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated user corrections, error-resolution patterns, repeated workflows and project-specific versus general behavioral distinctions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: base mode — background Observer identifies pattern/content/scope and updates an instinct; parent mode — user selects a project instinct for promotion to global scope. - Decision owner: base mode — Observer/Haiku agent; parent mode — human user for explicit promotion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool-use observation hooks, project detection, observation logs, confidence scoring, observer schedule, instinct stores and SessionStart injection. - Closure path: operational evidence → observer pattern judgment → persistent instinct → later SessionStart injection → subsequent agent behavior changes; parent promotion can widen a selected instinct&#39;s future scope. - Boundary reachability: continuous-learning-v2 ships the hooks, observer agent/loop, instinct store/CLI and SessionStart injection; the background mode is explicitly supported when enabled. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic capture/thresholds support the loop, while the observer model decides the learned pattern; the learned result is then automatically returned into later context. - Evidence: skills/continuous-learning-v2/SKILL.md, skills/continuous-learning-v2/agents/observer.md, scripts/hooks/session-start.js. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: observer defaults to disabled and is not viable as a persistent background process on native Windows; the A claim is for the supported enabled mode. - External distinction: user corrections, observed failures/resolutions and repeated workflows/tool preferences captured from real prior sessions. - Future / prospective distinction: whether those observed patterns should become reusable behavior in subsequent sessions/projects rather than only solve the current task. - Adaptation option generated: evidence-backed project/global instincts with a trigger, action, scope and confidence; related instincts can additionally be evolved/promoted. - Path back into current capability / S3: qualifying instincts are automatically injected at later SessionStart, changing the behavioral context available to future S1 work; parent promotion can widen their scope.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the reviewed ECC project/runtime recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying identity/constitutional disturbance is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5 publication. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: consent/safety boundaries, profiles, permissions, rules, security hooks, governance capture, learned preferences and operator configuration. - Closure path: no standard path is established from a genuine identity/ultimate-policy matter through authoritative S5 judgment back into governing whole-system operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ECC&#39;s policy surfaces regulate tasks/tools/review/security but do not establish an identity/constitutional authority organ at this recursion. - Evidence: README.md, skills/autonomous-agent-harness/SKILL.md, skills/continuous-learning-v2/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit absence after function-first review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream organization can impose an identity/constitution using ECC primitives; that separate system requires its own assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/feba4db663392fe60ec2b4e2f4f6633748c0c1a3/assessments/ecc.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit feba4db663392fe60ec2b4e2f4f6633748c0c1a3\">feba4db &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-18T00:57:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kingbootoshi/codex-orchestrator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Codex Orchestrator</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-18\">2026-11-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kingbootoshi/codex-orchestrator/tree/035d5813a1d93c4d8385ee4d5ff09a9416f6a749\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 035d5813a1d93c4d8385ee4d5ff09a9416f6a749\">035d581 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform project research, changes and test work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Varying task goals, source state, tool results and failed attempts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Worker chooses permissible code, investigation and tool actions. - Decision owner: Model-backed Codex worker in its interactive job. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Launcher, runtime environment, provided scope and turn signal. - Closure path: The worker reports a result or blocker. Its session stays available for corrections and another turn. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The plugin and CLI launch executable native workers rather than simulating task selection. - Evidence: Shipped Claude role · Worker turn and return loop · Native Codex launch - Basis: Explicit and structural. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: The native reasoning and tool runtime belong to Codex. - Boundary reachability: CLI start and skill delegation reach a live Codex process in a job-specific tmux session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Avoid duplicate modules and overlapping project stages between active cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Several Claude/Codex cells simultaneously target the same codebase. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Claim a distinct stage or module and use peers&#39; logged claims to adjust what to delegate. - Decision owner: Each involved Claude agent in the multi-session plugin mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Shared agents.log, job identifiers and a provided claim protocol. - Closure path: An existing stage or module claim is visible in the shared log. Peers partition their subsequent delegation accordingly. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The installed skill names the interference, tells agents to communicate a scope claim and leaves scope choice to them. - Evidence: Multi-session claims · Shipped Claude role - Basis: Explicit procedural and structural. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Advisory file convention. The CLI does not enforce exclusion or resolve simultaneous writes. - Boundary reachability: The project-wide claim convention and log format are shipped with the installed directing skill. - Distinct S1 units: Separately directed Claude/Codex cells with their own workers and project assignments. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Duplicate work or conflicting edits when two cells pick the same stage or module. - Attenuating coordination relation: Each directing agent claims a stage or module in the shared agents.log and reads other cells&#39; commitments. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The peer adjusts the next work allocation away from already claimed project scope. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The protocol ties advertised scope to duplicate-work and collision avoidance in a shared checkout.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate outstanding work across a worker portfolio and intervene in current commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stalled workers, contradictory findings, failing implementations and work split affecting the same project. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Use full portfolio state to choose which work starts, is redirected, split, continued or terminated. - Decision owner: Shipped Claude directing role in plugin mode, or the human CLI operator in a distinct direct mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Jobs/status/capture, per-turn completion signals and start/send/stop commands. - Closure path: A director reads the job set and output, changes the affected worker commitments and reads later status or output from the resulting work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The CLI supplies evidence and actuators. The plugin directs the Claude agent to exercise the portfolio decisions itself, while direct CLI mode leaves them with the user. - Evidence: Shipped Claude role · Plugin install mode · Current job portfolio · Job changes and follow-up · Project intervention instructions · Direct CLI controls - Basis: Explicit and structural. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Local worker portfolio only. Multiple Claude groups need additional partitioning to assure a single whole-project view. - Boundary reachability: Installer exposes the skill that gives Claude the live portfolio and command loop. The same CLI is directly installable and usable by an operator without that skill. - Whole-system current view: Job states, scope/cwd, turns, blockers, last output and usage across the managed portfolio, with capture for individual jobs. - Current-control decision scope: Assign current work, change job guidance, split failed work and release or terminate a worker commitment.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Provide complementary independent audit of live operational claims. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Worker summaries can overlook problems or misstate quality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separate source-witnessed operational audit mandate and corrective return path was established. - Decision owner: Read-only review workers and the directing agent own ordinary implementation review tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Review phase, worker logs, capture and status signals. - Closure path: Regular findings enter the pre-commit fix and testing process. Diagnostic capture feeds work steering. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Distinct review sessions are real, but their routine artifact checkpoint is part of the documented production pipeline. - Evidence: Production review stage · Pipeline completion criteria · Project intervention instructions · Current job portfolio - Basis: Bounded structural absence. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: A future or custom campaign can define sporadic organization-level sampling.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Prospectively model the external environment and update the campaign&#39;s own operating capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changes in tools, dependencies or the project context could require future adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped prospective capability decision and return workflow was established. - Decision owner: Claude directs current project ideation and research. The user decides ordinary project scope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Source mapping, project research, PRD and decision feedback from workers. - Closure path: Research and planning become implementation work for the current user request. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewed shipped path improves task planning. Its research results do not enter a prospective reconfiguration loop for the host organization. - Evidence: Task discovery and planning · Plugin install mode · Project intervention instructions - Basis: Bounded structural absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Users can formulate prospective projects, which are separate task instantiations.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Adjudicate changes to ultimate policy or campaign identity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User planning approval and project scope could conflict with working conventions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No reachable identity-policy dispute, ultimate decision and binding return path was established. - Decision owner: User sets the work and approves important plans. Skill defines the recurring operating rules. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: User discussion, PRD review, worker launch and session controls. - Closure path: Approval authorizes the implementation plan. The same strategic and worker authority pattern governs work afterward. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The task sponsor&#39;s approval updates the project plan. The supplied workflow lacks an adjudication path for the identity or ultimate rules of the directing organization. - Evidence: Shipped Claude role · Task discovery and planning · Pipeline completion criteria - Basis: Bounded structural absence. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: The user&#39;s project authority is explicit, with no automatic credit for policy-level closure at the assessment recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/fcba9c0177673838549647c205db16b2017f7df8/assessments/co/codex-orchestrator.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit fcba9c0177673838549647c205db16b2017f7df8\">fcba9c0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T11:23:08+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenCode Orchestrator</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/tree/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2\">fdba1b1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform code, test and project changes using an actual OpenCode backend agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing source, ambiguous instructions, failing tests and tool feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select which files to inspect/edit, what tests to run and what fix satisfies the task. - Decision owner: model-backed Commander working directly or separately invoked Worker; plugin only routes tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OpenCode host, tool adapters, session registry, mission memory and verification checklist. - Closure path: user task → Commander/Worker model tool actions → code/tests/evidence → reviewed mission result. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves prompt/routing code but no task-specific implementation choice. - Evidence: https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/commander.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/subagents/worker.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/tools/parallel/delegate-task.ts - Basis: shipped model agent definitions and runtime delegation path. - Confidence: high for supported mode; provider performance unmeasured. - Caveats: standalone question-only runs do not exhibit a coding operational cell. - Boundary reachability: published OpenCode 2 plugin adds Commander; Worker is a supported optional delegate.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no concrete first-party cross-operational conflict resolution circuit was established for parallel workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independent worker file edits or interface changes may be incompatible when merged. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Commander assigns scopes/dependencies and can review results; no specific inter-S1 clash signal/repair relation shown. - Decision owner: Commander for current task delegation rather than a demonstrated S2-specific conflict decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: parallel task manager, agentConcurrency, task result tool, scoped worker prompts and session IDs. - Closure path: workers report results to Commander but a collision-specific balancing signal and returned repair are not evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: file ownership guidance and queue limits prevent some overlap but delegation alone is not specific inter-S1 oscillation regulation. - Evidence: https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/commander.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/core/agents/manager.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/tools/parallel/delegate-task.ts - Basis: bounded negative on parallel task lifecycle and Commander scope/dep guidance. - Confidence: medium; a real Commander could notice conflicts not proven as a repeatable shipped circuit. - Caveats: S3 whole-mission corrective work and S3 Reviewer findings are not automatically S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: direct a whole currently active project mission, allocate scoped model work and intervene when delegated commitments or verification stall. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfinished TODO leaves, dependencies, failed delegates, stagnant iterations and incomplete verification of overall goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Commander selects task decomposition, model delegation, dependency ordering, replanning, repair or final acceptance. - Decision owner: model-backed Commander with whole-mission goal and result view, not the mission-loop continuation hook. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mission TODO/status/sync issues, parallel task list/results, progress/stagnation guard, checklist and durable ledger. - Closure path: whole mission evidence and delegated status → Commander reassigns/replans or repairs → worker next action and updated TODO/verified completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses revised work and scope; deterministic retry/verification cannot produce design-specific corrective decisions. - Evidence: https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/commander.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/core/loop/mission-loop-handler.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/prompts/common.ts - Basis: first-party model role and continuing mission control feedback. - Confidence: medium-high for selected active mission mode. - Caveats: a trivial question or single direct edit has no multi-cell S3 demonstrated; budget/loop enforcement alone is not ownership. - Whole-system current view: Commander sees entire user goal, all mission TODO leaves, child task status/results and aggregate verification requirements. - Current-control decision scope: assign Workers/Planner/Reviewer, choose serial dependency order, take over blocked work, re-plan and decide evidence-backed completion. - Boundary reachability: normal Commander and /task mode with optional delegatetask, task-list and result tools.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a Worker/Commander completion claim by checking actual project files and tests. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false success, broken interface contracts, untested regressions and incomplete TODO verification. - Decisive decision or feedback right: dedicated Reviewer model decides which checks to rerun and what findings block completion. - Decision owner: fresh role-specific Reviewer agent, not original implementer or deterministic checklist parser. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Reviewer prompt and scope, actual source/tests, mission checklist, sync-issues and returned task result. - Closure path: implementation result → Reviewer source/test checks → evidenced pass/fail/uncovered area → Commander repair or verified TODO completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reviewer makes code-specific fault judgments rather than only checking a manifest flag. - Evidence: https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/subagents/reviewer.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/prompts/common.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/core/loop/verification.ts - Basis: supported optional role-separated reviewer and executable evidence-return contract. - Confidence: medium-high for configured review; no paid model E2E validation. - Caveats: optional delegation, possibly same provider and no external institutional independence. - Claim being audited: Worker or Commander claims an implementation is complete and matches acceptance checks. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer result, task status and self-reported tests. - Complementary access path: separate Reviewer reads actual affected files, interfaces and executes project checks independently. - Independence boundary: distinct Reviewer invocation and independent direct source/tests, not a separate organization. - Who acts on findings: Commander updates scope or repairs; Reviewer may mark only verified assigned checklist leaves and log integration issues. - Boundary reachability: published plugin optional Reviewer subagent available via delegatetask.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no future external-environment adaptation loop into mission capability control was proven. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider changes, future market/user requirements and long-term technical alternatives. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Planner and Commander choose current technical steps, not enduring environmental adaptation options. - Decision owner: user/project leadership for long-horizon adaptation outside plugin. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Planner, mission memory, persistent TODO and model tools. - Closure path: current planning updates task implementation but no external future options negotiation with S3 is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an implementation plan or research tool call alone is not future-facing S4. - Evidence: https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/subagents/planner.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/core/knowledge/mission-memory.ts - Basis: negative path review of Planner, mission continuation and memory. - Confidence: medium-high at package boundary. - Caveats: a user can prompt strategic research, which does not demonstrate shipped S4 governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate identity-level policy decision loop is wired into the packaged OpenCode mission plugin. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user permission, mission replacement, project policy and final action boundaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human sets goals, stops or replaces missions, and approves operations as needed. - Decision owner: user/deployment parent for policy; plugin only enforces selected task permissions and state. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stop/cancel commands, prompts, bounded concurrency, session replacement and verification gates. - Closure path: human can cancel or redirect a mission, but an identity-level exception is not routed to ultimate authority and returned as governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: system prompt constraints and user approvals over coding steps are not S5 identity closure. - Evidence: https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/commander.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/core/loop/mission-loop.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/README.md - Basis: bounded negative across commands, safety, mission replacement, prompts and user rights. - Confidence: medium-high for installed plugin. - Caveats: maintainer governance or an external company policy engine is outside the system-in-focus.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3ac4ecb7a0a5204143934e584720845a924425ea/assessments/op/opencode-orchestrator.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3ac4ecb7a0a5204143934e584720845a924425ea\">3ac4ecb &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T00:24:53+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aden-hive/hive\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hive</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aden-hive/hive/tree/0c387492067e8b7d3e1c803009169f202f30ed77\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0c387492067e8b7d3e1c803009169f202f30ed77\">0c38749 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: Queen/workers autonomously perform outcome-producing business-process work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: the standard colony supplies agent-owned coordination through its shared ledger/plan, worker-report event path and Queen-driven convergence, providing concrete task-ownership and fleet-awareness mechanisms rather than simple one-shot delegation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the persistent Queen has a colony-wide current view through the plan/tracker and explicit authority to assign/fan out work, inspect and validate worker progress/results, converge execution and escalate exceptions. This is ongoing whole-colony current regulation rather than a component merely named manager.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: Queen-side validation uses the ordinary lead/control path and the human Sentinel is an escalation mechanism; the reviewed standard distribution does not establish a distinct sufficiently independent autonomous complementary-audit path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: reflexion, memory and learned skills improve operation over time, but learning ≠ a distinct external/prospective intelligence function coupled to S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: Queen personas, behavior triggers, HITL and cost controls shape operation, but legitimate ultimate task/policy authority remains with the user/configuration rather than an agent-owned S5 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/961323b8cd7cc12dfe8f927e44f355c2116c9944/assessments/hive.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 961323b8cd7cc12dfe8f927e44f355c2116c9944\">961323b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T23:12:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/1jehuang/jcode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">jcode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/1jehuang/jcode/tree/5f33d6239b56b3d381d6b2c0e9b151eef0b54cbe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5f33d6239b56b3d381d6b2c0e9b151eef0b54cbe\">5f33d62 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. jcode owns autonomous coding-agent sessions that execute repository work and can spawn/participate in swarm work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: A. The shared live plan carries explicit blockers, assignments and task state; worker outcomes and plan mutations change which nodes become runnable and which agent receives later work. Dependency and gate state therefore regulates inter-S1 ordering/conflict rather than merely routing messages.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: A. The coordinator owns the current shared plan and worker population, assigns/spawns work, observes progress/heartbeats and reclaims stranded assignments when workers die. This supplies autonomous whole-swarm current regulation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. Gate/verify node kinds and artifact checks occur inside the normal swarm execution path; no materially independent reviewer/auditor with distinct access and corrective authority was established at the pin.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. Ambient Mode proactively inspects recent project activity and can launch useful maintenance work, while its memory learns from approvals/rejections. That is ongoing S1 work plus internal experiential memory, not a distinct outside-looking prospective loop that develops and feeds changed organizational capability into current regulation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. The safety/approval architecture at the pin is explicitly a design document; moreover action permission by itself is not identity/ultimate-policy closure. No implemented S5 tension-resolution loop was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/aa0f4fc244d04c276678f55ed941376422a344af/assessments/jcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit aa0f4fc244d04c276678f55ed941376422a344af\">aa0f4fc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-14T00:30:16+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/akmatori/akmatori\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Akmatori</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-14\">2026-11-14</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/akmatori/akmatori/tree/be62d926fe6d92dceb5cf12e9a95cb587433cdf1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision be62d926fe6d92dceb5cf12e9a95cb587433cdf1\">be62d92 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Investigate and diagnose infrastructure incidents using agent-selected tool interactions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Diverse alerts, changing telemetry, uncertain root causes, tool errors and inconsistent infrastructure responses. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The dispatched investigation agent chooses diagnostic hypotheses, tool calls and proposed corrective steps within its tool permissions. - Decision owner: The incident-scoped autonomous agent running through the first-party worker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WebSocket orchestration, MCP gateway, incident database, connectors, tool allowlists, monitoring feeds and remediation proposal gates. - Closure path: Tool outputs and incident facts update the investigation; the agent returns a diagnosis, action proposal or resolution, while operator-gated remediation is applied only after separate permission. - Boundary reachability: Supported self-hosted standard deployment runs the first-party agent worker from incident intake; its decisions are not merely a repository example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the agent leaves incident capture and connector code, but not task-specific investigation, diagnosis and proposed remediation choices. - Evidence: README#L20-L43, agent-runner.ts, alertprocessor.go. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: External infrastructure tools and the LLM model provider remain dependencies. This does not assert unsupervised authorization of high-risk remediation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate conflict and duplication among incident-scoped S1 investigation units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Related alerts can spawn redundant investigations with inconsistent incident ownership or duplicate work; completed investigations can duplicate the same root-cause incident. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A first-party invoked LLM correlator decides whether a new alert belongs with a recent incident; supported opt-in merger separately chooses whether two already investigated incidents share a root cause. - Decision owner: LLM-based correlation/merge agent in the shipped service; code checks confidence thresholds, existing UUIDs, candidate status and admissible state transitions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Fingerprint fast-path, candidate incident selection, correlationThreshold, database relationships, status gates, incident attach/reparent transitions. - Closure path: A confident correlation attaches the new alert to the selected incident instead of creating another standalone investigation; opt-in post-investigation merge reparents alerts and retains a surviving incident whose later memory extraction reads the incident family. - Boundary reachability: AlertCorrelator is connected to normal alert intake, with enabled-by-default configuration; the additional incident merger is constructed by the normal server and activated by an explicit supported operator setting. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic fingerprint dedup alone is not the proof. Removing the one-shot model eliminates semantic judgment over distinct alert evidence and diagnosed root causes, while fixed gates only enforce admissibility. - Distinct S1 units: Independently spawned incident investigation agents, each with own incident scope and evidence record; the optional merger handles already-investigated separate incidents. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Redundant or overlapping investigations for one underlying failure can compete for incident identity and produce contradictory downstream work or memory. - Attenuating coordination relation: Semantic incoming-alert-to-live-incident correlation plus optional post-investigation cross-incident merging. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: New alerts follow an existing investigation context, while merged incidents stop being separate operational histories and are included in survivor memory/evidence processing. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The coordination decision resolves evidenced overlap in independent incident work instead of merely assigning a job to a worker or relaying a message. - Evidence: alertcorrelator.go#L134-L247, alertprocessor.go#L108-L158, incidentmerger.go, incidentmergertest.go#L67-L117, modelssettings.go#L250-L353. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Default correlation and optional post-investigation merge are separate modes. Merge is disabled by default; neither it nor a semantic correlation decision proves the effectiveness of the resulting incident diagnosis in production.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Regulate current whole-system incident fleet commitments, budgets and interventions on behalf of multiple operational investigations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent incidents, operator response capacity, pending remediations and incident lifecycle failures could demand whole-system present control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party whole-system S3 decision path, autonomous or parent-governed, was established from the inspected incident and approval services. - Decision owner: Operators decide isolated remediation approvals, while runtime schedulers and status services enforce local rules; no separate agent-owned whole-fleet S3 decision right evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Incident state transitions, approval queue, worker orchestration, monitor sweeps, event ingestion, cron schedules and tool permission gates. - Closure path: Local incident interventions change local state, but no evidenced first-party choice reallocates whole-fleet current commitments on a shared system view. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Dispatch, scheduling and approval of a single risky action are not the organizational whole-system control right described by S3. - Whole-system current view: Operator dashboards show incidents and settings, but an integrated first-party decision/return path over multiple fleet commitments was not identified. - Current-control decision scope: No evidenced actor renegotiates priorities or resources for the current incident-response organization as a whole. - Evidence: incidentservice.go, proposalservice.go, agent orchestrator. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Provide a complementary post-incident evidentiary check of operational diagnosis and claimed resolution, distinct from routine investigator self-report. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An investigator may present an unsupported causal claim, incorrect timeline or false recovery assertion as a durable fact for the next incident. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separate post-closure LLM extraction pass compares operational facts, original alerts, tool outputs, timestamps and agent claims, then selects which durable observed facts and feedback should become memory. - Decision owner: The independently invoked post-incident extraction model makes the evidence selection/judgment; Go service validates schema and assigns evidence grades, always leaving causal assertions unverified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: On-close extraction queue and wake-up, independent one-shot LLM caller, input redaction, evidence-grade restrictions, transactional memory writes, SKILL.md manifest refresh. - Closure path: Separate extraction generates graded memories, writes them after closure with stale-claim guards, and refreshes manifests read by future incident agents, changing subsequent investigation context. - Boundary reachability: Default server startup wires NewIncidentMemoryExtractor, close-path kickers and the extractor Run loop; this is an active first-party path, not a test or blog vignette. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the separate extraction model leaves the original investigation self-report and fixed storage rules but not an independent semantic account of what was actually observed. - Claim being audited: The original agent&#39;s root-cause story, resolved status and potential reusable observations are grounded in incident reality. - Ordinary reporting path: The incident investigator&#39;s final response and run transcript. - Complementary access path: Original alert text, incident timeline and close reason, finished run logs/tool outputs, operator feedback and related incident family evidence. - Independence boundary: A separate post-incident model pass receives original artifacts in addition to the producing agent&#39;s answer; it distinguishes self-report from observed recovery, and code prevents it from declaring causal claims verified. - Who acts on findings: MemoryService writes the graded output, then SkillService refreshes future agent context. This is a retrospective corrective feedback channel, not a remediation-veto gate. - Evidence: extraction prompt, evidence apply, extractor process, runtime wiring, worker start. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Independence is claim-relative; factual labeling and separate model reading are not a guarantee of true ground truth. No runtime test of future agent reliance on the graded memory was run for this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: Analyze environmental incident patterns and operator feedback to propose future adaptations of response capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Repeated failed/slow investigations, changed infrastructure symptoms, missing runbooks and ineffective skill instructions threaten future diagnostic fitness. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Opt-in improvement-evaluator agent proposes runbook/memory/cron/skill changes; a legitimate operator approves, edits or rejects the adaptation and owns the final decision. - Decision owner: Operator parent for the decisive adaptation authorization; the evaluator is a first-party option generator, not the owner of final strategy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Seeded improvement-evaluator cron, incident/feedback retrieval tools, proposal gateway, staleness checks, review UI and first-party runbook/memory/cron/skill managers. - Closure path: Operator-approved proposal is applied through managers and changes subsequent runs, runbooks, memory or permissible skills; rejected proposal leaves operating capability unchanged. - Boundary reachability: First-party startup seeds the evaluator in a disabled-by-default but operator-enableable mode, and the existing proposal service can apply an approved proposed asset change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent generates future options, but their adoption is reserved for the legitimate operator and remains unavailable without that parent decision. - External distinction: Real incident histories, monitoring failures, different infrastructure conditions and operator feedback from actual work. - Future / prospective distinction: Future response correctness, investigation latency and robustness of next incidents rather than repair of only the current alert. - Adaptation option generated: New/updated runbook, memory, recurring cron task or non-system skill prompt. - Path back into current capability / S3: Proposal review and apply update operational assets for later investigations; the operator closes the adaptation against currently authorized response capability rather than the software autonomously rewriting its mandate. - Evidence: improvement evaluator seed, proposal service approval, proposal application, proposal first-party tool. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: This is an opt-in mode: seed configuration is Enabled=false and toolsenabled=false. The assessment does not claim autonomous A, regular self-improvement on every installation, or a successful observed human approval in a production deployment.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Resolve ultimate identity/policy disputes and authoritatively return those decisions into subsequent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Proposed changes to enduring mission, ultimate acceptable-remediation bounds or constitution of the agent organization could create identity-level conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped identity-level escalation and authoritative return was established in the operating boundary. - Decision owner: Operators and maintainers set policy externally; routine approval of an action and protected system-skill settings are not evidence of a first-party S5 owner/closure loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool authorization, proposal approval gates, administrator settings, system-skill protection and human-reviewed remediation. - Closure path: Ordinary approvals apply or reject individual operations and S4 improvement proposals, not constitution-level changes that are returned as new system identity. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent and operator decisions are limited to tasks and permitted capability changes; an ultimate-policy decision path is not shipped. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Who may change the organization-level purpose, hard risk boundaries or constitutional governance rules of the self-hosted response organization. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: No first-party positive S5 mode; external human/maintainer ultimate authority cannot be borrowed without a function-specific returned decision path. - Return-to-operation path: No traced identity-level escalation → legitimate parent judgment → revised policy governing the later operational runtime. - Evidence: proposalservice.go, proposals.go, modelssettings.go. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/01a6d9744616ae2c33f08569f9669fd1383cce78/assessments/ak/akmatori.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 01a6d9744616ae2c33f08569f9669fd1383cce78\">01a6d97 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-20T17:50:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cogitator-ai/Cogitator-AI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Cogitator</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-20\">2026-11-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cogitator-ai/Cogitator-AI/tree/67b4fde644f648bc1df02a1ab38b211cf2171fbd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 67b4fde644f648bc1df02a1ab38b211cf2171fbd\">67b4fde &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce user/task deliverables by selecting model-driven tool calls and revising the response to task/environment evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New user inputs, tool results, retrieval, execution errors and unforeseen partial subtask outputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select tool/arguments, incorporate result, and decide the next task action or final output. - Decision owner: Active Agent model in a configured Cogitator run, or respective model worker inside a swarm. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model/provider adapter, typed Zod tools, execution loop, history and context builder, permission engine and budget checks. - Closure path: Owner-supplied goal -&gt; runtime invokes model -&gt; model-selected guarded tool executes -&gt; tool result re-enters model history -&gt; model revises action or final answer. - Boundary reachability: Public new Agent, new Cogitator, cog.run in shipped core; examples instantiate genuine models and tool functions in the supported API. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Runtime enforces typed/permission rules but does not decide the substantive answer. The configured model does, without implying first-party model hosting. - Evidence: README quick start, agent class, runtime, tool execution. - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate inter-agent competition over shared tasks and incompatible decisions, coordinating distinct autonomous worker loops. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two agents claiming one task, taking blocked work, incompatible proposal choices, duplicate work and dependency conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Lead model assigns cross-agent roles/work, updates assignments from board state, and in consensus mode model voters can revise proposals during subsequent rounds; deterministic board guard only prevents invalid claims. - Decision owner: Model lead for discretionary team coordination and participating model voters for inter-agent consensus; board atomically enforces claim rights under configured team owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TeamTaskBoard.claimTask checks synchronous state, dependency/owner constraints and one active task per member; blackboard events, mailboxes, assignment tools, consensus ballot/round policy. - Closure path: Lead creates/assigns tasks -&gt; workers see and claim eligible tasks -&gt; board prevents competing claims and emits completion/message events -&gt; lead/other agents see updated board or votes -&gt; follow-up assignments, conflict resolution and work choices change. - Boundary reachability: First-party @cogitator-ai/swarms team, hierarchical and consensus strategies in published package; app owner composes the agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The board itself is an enforcing data structure; agent-owned part is the cross-worker discretionary assignment and decision/feedback inside team or vote reasoning. - Distinct S1 units: At least two workers, each with a separate Agent model conversation and tools. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Contention for one task and changed dependencies on the shared board; disagreement among independent model voters. - Attenuating coordination relation: Atomic board claims plus team lead and consensus voting/discussion bounds. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: listtasks, readinbox, completion/message events and next-round vote context alter subsequent worker/lead action selection. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Built-in concurrency-safe claim arbitration and explicit conflict-convergence rounds regulate mutually interfering independent workers, not only pass messages. - Evidence: board, team tools, team strategy, consensus, swarm specification. - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Manage whole current-task commitments across team members, dependencies, failed/pending work and integration decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incomplete dependencies, worker stalls/failures, unclaimed tasks, competing priorities and partial output. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model lead creates work items, sets dependencies, assigns/reassigns work, requests revisions and synthesizes accepted outputs in response to current whole-team status. - Decision owner: Model leader in supported team or hierarchical supervisor mode; runtime scheduler and owner-configured max turns/budgets are enforcing supports. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model-visible createtask, assigntask, listtasks, requestrevision, blackboard, per-member turn status and result visibility. - Closure path: Read full current board/results -&gt; lead model chooses reallocation/revision or new task -&gt; board and worker calls change in-flight current work -&gt; completion/event/result returns -&gt; lead revises or concludes. - Boundary reachability: TeamStrategy.execute ensures a lead agent takes the initial and return turns; HierarchicalStrategy supplies requestrevision and checkprogress and returns final supervisor output. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The lead model makes current system-level priority/acceptance judgments; worker-queue scheduling and fixed budget ceilings alone would not establish S3. - Whole-system current view: Live task board with owner, dependencies, status, results and failures for a single user-task team. - Current-control decision scope: Replan, reassign, request revisions, integrate or escalate within the owner-declared goal; not resource governance across company deployments. - Evidence: team, board, tools, hierarchical, sample first-party run. - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Construct a second model-operated reviewer path that challenges draft/worker factual claims before whole-task integration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A writer&#39;s unverified factual claims might be accepted as final merely because its own report says complete. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Separate configured reviewer Agent inspects a worker draft and returns wrong/unsupported/correct findings; team lead consumes review task result and can require revisions. - Decision owner: Separately instantiated reviewer model for substantive content verdict, lead model for integration and rework. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party team task/dependency board, distinct worker message histories, updatetask return, optional reviewer assignment and source-check tools. - Closure path: Parent creates draft/review tasks with dependency -&gt; author completes draft -&gt; reviewer gets draft and available sources -&gt; reviewer records independent assessment -&gt; leader sees reviewer task result -&gt; revision request or changed output. - Boundary reachability: Published first-party team constructor has a checker model role in examples/swarms/05-agent-team.ts, which explicitly checks factual support and returns a separate verdict; @cogitator-ai/swarms can compose such a role at runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: When composed, the reviewer itself decides factual support separately from the author; ordinary eval/unit tests and dashboard traces do not count. - Claim being audited: A separate writer&#39;s factual claims in a draft. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker writer composes output and marks its task completed. - Complementary access path: Reviewer receives the draft and separately supplied source/search tools, not only the author&#39;s self-grading; source access and role isolation must be configured for the instance. - Independence boundary: Distinct model agent and separate task role but no guarantee of different model/vendor, blinded evidence, or independent upstream auditor. Specific first-party construction seam exists; actual audits remain contingent on composition. - Who acts on findings: Team lead accepts/reworks the draft using the returned checker task verdict. - Evidence: checker role + task team, team board and task results, team tools, hierarchical revision path. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: medium - Caveats: C rather than A because the source packages primitives and a working example but not a guaranteed always-on reviewer for arbitrary deployments. Reviewer&#39;s raw evidence access is app-configured; source inspection does not prove real model-independent execution.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No demonstrable standard first-party loop independently scans external future uncertainty, selects prospective capability options and returns implemented adaptation to current organizational operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New external opportunities/threats and long-run changes to demand, models, technology and operating context could affect future capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Self-modifying GapAnalyzer uses the current user&#39;s intent and failed attempts to propose task-local tools; ObservationCollector measures current-run progress/stagnation and meta-reasoning switches current-run strategy. External future-directed intelligence and authority are not established. - Decision owner: User/application integrator for persistent architecture/identity choices; none for a separately qualified S4 process in the assessed standard system. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Self-modifying tool generation, local observation and architecture optimizer, memory upkeep, dynamic model selection, web retrieval and experimental scheduling. - Closure path: Current task-gap -&gt; generated tool or strategy setting -&gt; current task can improve. This is a current-operation adjustment and does not establish external-and-then environmental intelligence returned as sustained new capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Internal parameter optimization and tactical tool generation are not automatically S4; memory of a previous chat and external web lookup for a present question are still operational. - Evidence: self modifying, gap analyzer, run observations, architecture selection, core memory. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party agent-owned or closed parent-governed ultimate identity/organizational purpose decision-and-return loop is established at the generic deployed task-team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Contradictory operating goals, changes to legitimate organizational mission, and high-priority identity commitments require an ultimate-policy owner. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Host/application developer configures agent instructions, approvers, execution permissions, providers and team roles; approvals on tools/workflow nodes are tactical safety/control decisions, not proven mission identity governance. - Decision owner: External application&#39;s human owner. Inside the library runtime no sourced S5-specific parent-return mechanism was established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permission modes and approval UI, config files, human workflow nodes, scoped role instructions, optional memory. - Closure path: Config changes can affect later runs, but no first-party structured identity/policy issue -&gt; legitimate ultimate decision -&gt; accepted revision -&gt; operational feedback loop was shown for this recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model may set tasks and choose tools, but model autonomy within supplied instructions does not authorize redefining the enterprise/personal identity policy. Generic pause/approve is not S5. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: User-defined foundational goal and enduring authority hierarchy, not the correctness of one tool execution. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: No positive S5 mode claimed; developer/operator holds the external application policy. - Return-to-operation path: Host-supplied prompt/config affects future calls, but without a demonstrated first-party governance loop for legitimate identity changes. - Evidence: workflow approvals, runtime security, architecture, memory. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3e85a14ccb7e76181fe78fa73b80ce1f4f1ff003/assessments/co/cogitator.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3e85a14ccb7e76181fe78fa73b80ce1f4f1ff003\">3e85a14 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T22:03:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AntigmaLabs/ante\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ante</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-21\">2026-09-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AntigmaLabs/ante/tree/74ce24554341fb0c6cc3521baa5da5b94cedf881\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 74ce24554341fb0c6cc3521baa5da5b94cedf881\">74ce245 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform user-directed software-engineering work through a repeated model/tool feedback loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous coding requests, repository state, file/shell/tool observations, execution failures, model/context limits and delegated-work results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive next actions/tools/delegation and decide when enough operational evidence exists to return a result. - Decision owner: the running root model agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: daemon/session turn lifecycle, tool implementations, permission engine, provider transport, persistence, compaction and external/local inference hosts. - Closure path: user task → model decision → first-party tool/delegation execution → returned observation → subsequent model decision → completed task/result. - Boundary reachability: the shipped Ante TUI/headless/server paths instantiate this model/tool loop directly; no downstream harness composition is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime mechanisms execute and constrain actions, but the model owns the substantive selection of what to do next and whether the task is operationally complete. - Evidence: docs-site/docs/reference/architecture.mdx, docs-site/docs/reference/tools-reference.mdx, README headless/TUI usage. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model inference may be external or local; ownership is credited to the model actor operating through Ante&#39;s first-party loop, not to the provider.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate contention among concurrently delegated Ante work units for bounded shared inference capacity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple child agents attempting concurrent model work when the selected local/model-serving environment can only process a smaller number of requests without queueing/timeouts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine how much concurrent subagent work the session may admit before excess work waits. - Decision owner: constructor/operator configuration; Ante supplies the function-specific concurrency mechanism but no autonomous agent owns or revises the cap in the documented standard mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxconcurrentsubagents, waiting-for-slot queueing and parent interruption/cancellation behavior. - Closure path: parallel child calls → cap check → excess child waits → slot becomes available → queued child starts; later S1 execution therefore changes according to the coordination result. - Boundary reachability: the cap is a documented built-in session setting applied directly to built-in Agent calls in ordinary Ante execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the root chooses which subagents to delegate to, but the specific collision-attenuation policy is selected through settings and enforced by the runtime; an autonomous S2 decision owner is not supplied. - Evidence: docs-site/docs/extend/subagents.mdx, docs-site/docs/configuration/preference.mdx, docs-site/docs/reference/tools-reference.mdx. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic delegation is not credited as S2; only the documented shared-capacity interference and cap/queue relation are used. - Distinct S1 units: the root organization can launch multiple built-in/custom subagents that independently execute real delegated tasks with their own model context and tool set. - Inter-S1 disturbance: child model requests can exceed the usable concurrent capacity of the selected inference environment; the docs specifically call out a local model that handles one request at a time. - Attenuating coordination relation: maxconcurrentsubagents limits admitted parallel children and queues excess calls until a slot opens. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: queued child work does not begin until capacity becomes available, preventing the competing S1 requests from simultaneously consuming the bounded model-serving channel. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited relation is explicitly tied to regulating a concrete concurrency/capacity interference among multiple operational child units, not to the existence of the Agent handoff itself.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current session&#39;s continuing commitment against a declared success condition and intervene when progress is insufficient or the goal is no longer reachable. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete work, premature completion, current-turn outcomes that do not yet satisfy the session goal, and situations where the active commitment has become unreachable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: after each turn, judge whether the whole-session goal is met, unreachable or requires another regulated continuation with corrective feedback. - Decision owner: the model-based goal evaluator in the first-party /goal mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: goal-session protocol state, deterministic continuation plumbing, session persistence and iteration cap. - Closure path: current conversation + goal → evaluator judgment → continue returns evaluator feedback into another operational turn, while met/unreachable terminates the commitment. - Boundary reachability: /goal is a shipped first-party TUI/protocol capability and can be used directly without a downstream controller. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the iteration cap is only a backstop; the substantive assessment of current performance and continue/stop state is made by the evaluator model and its feedback changes subsequent work. - Evidence: docs-site/docs/usage/goal-sessions.mdx, protocol documentation and changelog entries for goal evaluation. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary one-turn execution alone would not establish S3; this state is credited from the supported goal-driven mode. - Whole-system current view: the goal evaluator receives the active success condition and evaluates the conversation after each completed turn rather than judging one isolated tool call. - Current-control decision scope: continue the current commitment with corrective feedback, or end it as met/unreachable; this regulates current performance/accountability for the session as a whole.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: independently challenge changed-code quality claims through multiple review lenses and return complementary findings for corrective action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplication, unnecessary complexity, avoidable inefficiency and misplaced abstraction that can survive the ordinary implementation/check path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each independent reviewer judges the same scoped changes under a distinct audit lens and returns supported findings without editing the work being audited. - Decision owner: constructor path; first-party curated simplify supplies the audit organization and reviewer contracts, but the standard Ante installation does not equip/invoke this lane automatically. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: skill discovery/equipping, parallel subagent calls, shared review scope, prohibition on reviewer edits, parent deduplication/validation and subsequent normal tool execution. - Closure path: implementation/diff → independent reviewer findings → parent validates/deduplicates → worthwhile corrections are applied and verified. - Boundary reachability: the skill is shipped in the public Ante repository and is installable through the documented first-party skill mechanism; it is therefore a concrete first-party constructor surface, not a hypothetical downstream design. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reviewer judgments are agentic, but obtaining this S3 organization still requires the operator/developer to install/equip the curated skill; Ante does not supply it as a resident standard audit owner. - Evidence: curated/skills/simplify/SKILL.md, curated/README.md, docs-site/docs/extend/skills.mdx. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary tests, /diff, logs and root self-review are not credited; only the separate reviewer construction path supports C. - Claim being audited: that the current changed code is sufficiently reusable, simple, efficient and well-abstracted for the requested cleanup scope. - Ordinary reporting path: the root agent implements/inspects the task and runs its ordinary checks. - Complementary access path: independent subagents receive the same diff/scope under separate reuse, simplification, efficiency and abstraction lenses and return findings without modifying files. - Independence boundary: reviewer contexts are separate subagents and are prohibited from editing; the parent receives their findings only after each independent judgment is made. - Who acts on findings: the parent agent validates and deduplicates findings, applies worthwhile improvements and runs verification.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: learn reusable distinctions from user/project experience and convert them into persistent guidance that changes later sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurring user corrections, confirmations, project constraints, learned working patterns and memories that become stale or wrong over time. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which observed lessons should be recorded, updated or removed as persistent project memory for future work. - Decision owner: the running Ante agent under the first-party auto-memory behavior. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-project memory directory, typed memory format, MEMORY.md cap, session-start memory injection and Write/Edit tools. - Closure path: current user/project interaction → agent identifies reusable lesson → memory is created/updated/removed → future session injects the retained memory into the system prompt → later operational choices change. - Boundary reachability: auto-memory is a documented built-in Ante capability enabled by default in interactive TUI sessions and optionally in headless mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the user supplies environmental feedback, but the agent is explicitly taught to consult, record, revise and discard persistent memories; the runtime only stores and reloads the selected adaptation. - Evidence: docs-site/docs/extend/memory.mdx, preference/headless documentation. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: session transcript persistence alone is not credited; the witness is the agent-selected conversion of experience into reusable future-session guidance. - External distinction: corrections, confirmations and project-specific guidance arrive from the user/project environment rather than from an internal fixed task plan alone. - Future / prospective distinction: the agent evaluates whether a lesson should guide later conversations and can remove or update it when no longer valid. - Adaptation option generated: persist or revise a concise typed memory/rule describing how future work should be handled. - Path back into current capability / S3: retained memories are automatically injected into later session prompts, changing the options and constraints available to subsequent S1 work and goal-regulated sessions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop is established at the selected Ante boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: operator-defined prompts, permissions, profiles and project settings constrain behavior, but no specific identity/ultimate-policy conflict or proposal is routed through an S5 closure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision path with complete issue → legitimate authority → authoritative decision → returned governance was established. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user systemprompt/appendsystemprompt, permission modes/rules, profiles, project settings and generic AskUser questions. - Closure path: configuration is loaded into sessions and generic questions can return user input, but no function-specific S5 issue-and-return path is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parent/operator authority over configuration is real, but Methodology 0.3.5 does not treat editable policy text, permission gates or generic human input as S5 by themselves. - Evidence: docs-site/docs/configuration/preference.mdx, docs-site/docs/configuration/permission.mdx, protocol/AskUser documentation. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a wider organization embedding Ante may provide S5, but that authority is outside this standalone harness boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/85d7c52a6a7a091b79de156dc8d709c08773eff1/assessments/ante.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 85d7c52a6a7a091b79de156dc8d709c08773eff1\">85d7c52 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-16T00:10:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Mng-dev-ai/agentrove\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agentrove</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-15\">2026-11-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Mng-dev-ai/agentrove/tree/a1cfe4999f50646b00dcab696483d1526071291b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a1cfe4999f50646b00dcab696483d1526071291b\">a1cfe49 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute an assigned coding or investigation task in a workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uneven codebase conditions, tool outcomes and the changing information needed to complete the assignment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose successive edits, tool actions and a task result in the configured execution mode. - Decision owner: The ACP coding agent operating as a workspace chat or worker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Workspace binding, ACP session and tool feedback, stream lifecycle and result persistence. - Closure path: The agent receives a prompt, acts in its bound workspace, receives tool feedback and returns a persisted completion or interruption to the user or lead. - Boundary reachability: The Docker distribution and first-party ACP launcher connect the agent to the workspace and its available tools after ordinary provisioning. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The running agent chooses task actions and when to return a result. - Evidence: ACP prompt and update loop, MCP runtime binding, worker launch contract. - Basis: Source reconstruction. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Models, credentials and supported tool permissions remain deployment prerequisites.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate collisions between agent units sharing a working directory. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two members can attempt overlapping file changes or redundant responses to the same developing discussion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose and announce an intended edit, yield when another member is working on it, or suppress a redundant response. - Decision owner: The participating agents in the channel. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The injected introduction gives a file-level avoidance rule; the channel incorporates agent replies into subsequent member prompts. - Closure path: An agent announces its edit scope. Subsequent deliveries expose that intent to other members, whose next work selection can exclude that scope. - Boundary reachability: First-party group channels install the prompt and delivery behavior on configured member sessions. - Distinct S1 units: Separate channel member agents with their own execution sessions. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Overlapping edits in the shared directory and repetitive turn responses. - Attenuating coordination relation: Before-edit announcement, peer avoidance and a reply-or-pass protocol. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Later prompts include the peer response and its stated work scope so a member can yield or choose another edit. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The shipped instructions explicitly connect each unit&#39;s intended edit to the other units&#39; next permissible work choices. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Members decide whether their proposed work overlaps an announced action and which action they take next. - Evidence: File-avoidance protocol, protocol injection and member execution, reply redistribution. - Basis: Source reconstruction. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: The coordination protocol depends on agents following the prompt. Simultaneous edits can still race before the announcements propagate.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate active worker commitments and interruptions across one live workspace assignment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stalled workers, conflicting or unfinished assignments, and the need to stop or redirect the group. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide whether a worker continues, gets redirected or stops in the lead mode. Decide how the channel&#39;s active commitments and membership change in the operator mode. - Decision owner: Lead agent in an MCP-delegated workgroup. The owning operator in a supervised group channel. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Lead-accessible chat roster, outcomes, pending requests, follow-up and cancellation. Channel activity and pending permissions, member controls, group stop and subsequent message dispatch. - Closure path: A finished or blocked turn wakes the lead. It can answer a pending request, stop a worker or revise its assignment. In the channel mode, operator intervention cancels active turns or changes membership, and the next committed prompt fans out over the resulting group. - Boundary reachability: Both the lead session&#39;s bundled MCP service and the channel owner&#39;s backend API and UI are ordinary supported features. - Whole-system current view: Lead sees the workgroup through its child roster, completion and interruption summaries, with drill-down into each worker. Channel owner sees participant activity and pending inputs in a shared view. - Current-control decision scope: Restart, stop or reassign an active commitment, including a group interruption and resumed work with changed membership or priorities. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The lead chooses the intervention in its delegated workgroup. The operator exercises the corresponding workgroup-level discretion in a supervised channel. - Evidence: MCP worker roster and controls, stop and pending-request controls, lead wake and feedback, owner-bound channel operations, group stop and delivery handling. - Basis: Source reconstruction and first-party wake test cases. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: Evidence covers one workgroup and the source-defined lead and operator modes. Matters requiring the user&#39;s judgement may still be escalated.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Independent inspection of ongoing operational claims and return to control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Reported progress or completion can diverge from the actual worktree or execution evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select a claim, inspect independent evidence and return an actionable judgment to workgroup control. - Decision owner: No audit-specific owner established in the shipped workgroup. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Chat history, worktrees and the external GitHub review interface expose potential evidence. - Closure path: No dedicated verdict and return path is installed for an audit role. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An agent reviewer requires a separately assigned mandate and evidence target. - Evidence: Documented reviewer-persona recipe, event logging, GitHub reviewer routing. - Basis: Bounded source inspection. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: A user or lead can compose an audit by creating a reviewer persona and assigning an independent inspection.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Sense relevant environmental change, choose prospective adaptations and install them into subsequent capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future work can face changed tools, systems and requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select a future-relevant change and alter reusable workgroup capability in response. - Decision owner: No adaptation owner established in the shipped workgroup. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Scheduled dispatch, persistent memories and persona editing supply general tools. - Closure path: No shipped trigger-to-adaptation path connects environmental change to a governed capability update. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A scheduled task author can compose the judgment and capability update. - Evidence: MCP memory and persona tools, memory prompt composition, scheduled dispatch. - Basis: Bounded source inspection. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: A configured scheduled prompt can instruct an agent to carry out an adaptation workflow.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Resolve workgroup identity or ultimate-policy decisions and return them to ongoing operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential conflict over the workgroup mission or the enduring boundary of its mandate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Settle the workgroup identity or ultimate policy and bind future operation to it. - Decision owner: No identity or ultimate-policy owner established in the assessed workgroup modes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Workspace provisioning, agent permissions, channel membership and ordinary query escalation. - Closure path: No workgroup identity decision and durable governance return were established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The lead agent may refer individual issues to the operator. The observed intervention path covers ordinary work. - Evidence: Channel permission and member controls, member configuration updates, lead escalation prompt. - Basis: Bounded source inspection. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: Operators retain workspace and model provisioning authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3e52bbde76a32562e611c236ee6eb478865484e8/assessments/ag/agentrove.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3e52bbde76a32562e611c236ee6eb478865484e8\">3e52bbd &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T22:13:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/coleam00/Linear-Coding-Agent-Harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Linear Coding Agent Harness</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/coleam00/Linear-Coding-Agent-Harness/tree/c37dc096993011cb0cc48a0afab176cd9c8f2375\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c37dc096993011cb0cc48a0afab176cd9c8f2375\">c37dc09 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: deliver working application features from a supplied specification. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code, failed behavior, tool errors and incomplete features. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose implementation actions, tests, fixes and verified feature completion. - Decision owner: initializer/coding model agents in the concrete shipped SDK composition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claude SDK tool loop, local coding tools, Linear MCP, Puppeteer, Bash allowlist and session driver. - Closure path: agent observes file/tool/browser results, fixes the implementation, commits work and records issue completion with evidence for the next session. - Boundary reachability: runautonomousagent loads the shipped prompts and createclient tool configuration directly; custom agent organization is unnecessary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime continuation cannot implement a feature without agent choices; supplied requirements constrain outcomes rather than every action. - Evidence: agent.py, client.py, prompts/codingprompt.md, prompts/initializerprompt.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded source inspection. - Caveats: Static supported-path assessment; no live model/provider execution or effectiveness claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: avoid duplicate/conflicting feature ownership among project-working sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two coding agents acting on the same feature and interrupted-session work being mistaken for free work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: interpret In Progress ownership, avoid another actor&#39;s claimed work and publish a claim before implementation. - Decision owner: an autonomous coordinating/peer arrangement still needs composition when multiple instances operate; the shipped serial instance and status convention alone do not close authoritative concurrent ownership. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Linear issue statuses, comments and per-session checks; no atomic compare-and-swap claim is asserted. - Closure path: claim/status and handoff become visible to later peer reads, changing issue eligibility; trustworthy attribution and concurrent conflict resolution remain composition obligations. - Boundary reachability: coding prompt Steps 2, 5, 6 and workflow prohibitions explicitly expose the collision-specific convention through configured Linear tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the function-specific feedback primitive exists, but multiple-instance authority/recovery is not fully wired; no S2=A claim. - Distinct S1 units: separately executing project feature sessions when the documented other-agent convention is used. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping work on an already claimed issue. - Attenuating coordination relation: publish In Progress and refrain from working another agent&#39;s In Progress issue. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: subsequent Linear status read directs the peer toward a different eligible issue or resumed own work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the cited rule explicitly regulates duplicate ownership rather than merely storing or routing tasks. - Evidence: prompts/codingprompt.md, client.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded source inspection. - Caveats: Serial default does not itself demonstrate concurrent regulation. The prompt also prioritizes interrupted In Progress work; distinguishing that from another active owner&#39;s claim is left to composition.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current commitments and project stability across the feature portfolio. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a previously reported completed feature regresses while new feature work competes for attention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reopen a completed feature, divert work to its repair before adding features and choose the next eligible priority from current project state. - Decision owner: current coding-session agent under the shipped project-wide role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Linear portfolio/status/META reads and writes, browser access and local tools; iteration cap merely bounds execution. - Closure path: directly observed regression causes Done→In Progress and repair; updated state/notes constrain subsequent feature selection and later sessions. - Boundary reachability: codingprompt Steps 2 and 4 run before Step 5; the concrete client supplies list/update/comment and raw app access in every normal continuation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent judges operational evidence and intervenes on project commitments; deterministic delay/cap does not make that judgment. - Whole-system current view: all project Done/Todo/In Progress counts, META progress, app specification, Git and sampled implemented behavior. - Current-control decision scope: project feature priorities, recovery of broken commitments and authority to suspend new feature development for repair. - Evidence: prompts/codingprompt.md, client.py, agent.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded source inspection. - Caveats: Static supported-path assessment; no live model/provider execution or effectiveness claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge predecessor session completion claims through complementary direct application inspection. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Linear Done/comments can remain positive while actual app behavior has regressed or was misreported. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose 1–2 previously Done core features, judge their current UI reality and invalidate a prior completion when contradicted. - Decision owner: fresh continuation coding agent, independent of the predecessor context being checked. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh SDK client, Linear claims, Puppeteer navigation/screenshots and issue update tools. - Closure path: a failed sampled check reopens the issue, records what broke and forces repair before new work; findings also enter META for later control. - Boundary reachability: the installed coding prompt mandates predecessor-feature checks before new implementation and client.py provides those browser/Linear tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the fresh agent makes the sampled audit judgment; tool output and status machinery only provide access and persistence. - Claim being audited: previous-session Done status and implementation/verification comments. - Ordinary reporting path: Linear issue statuses, implementation comments and META session summaries. - Complementary access path: direct browser operation and screenshots of selected already-completed core app features. - Independence boundary: fresh client/context checks predecessor claims without the predecessor&#39;s private conversation; same model family and serial role do not prove stronger institutional independence. - Who acts on findings: the current agent reopens/repairs and future sessions consume the changed portfolio. - Evidence: agent.py, prompts/codingprompt.md, client.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded source inspection. - Caveats: Credited only for the pre-work sample of predecessor Done claims. The same agent&#39;s Step 8 QA of its own new feature is ordinary production testing and is not additional S3. Static mandated path, not an observed audit run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: outside-and-then capability adaptation is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: only current application feature/defect requirements are regulated. - Decisive decision or feedback right: initializer planning and session pacing allocate existing application work, not prospective harness adaptation. - Decision owner: no function-specific first-party adaptation owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: app specification, META memory, feature priorities and browser feedback. - Closure path: no external/future distinctions→adaptation options→present harness capability return found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: project planning and bug recovery do not themselves close S4. - Evidence: prompts/initializerprompt.md, prompts/codingprompt.md, agent.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded source inspection. - Caveats: Static supported-path assessment; no live model/provider execution or effectiveness claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: identity/ultimate-policy closure is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: operational task safety and supplied application scope, without a policy-dispute loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: static role, app spec and command permissions constrain execution; no ultimate-policy adjudication path is shipped. - Decision owner: user/developer sets initial requirements and configuration; no runtime S5 owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SDK permissions, sandbox configuration, allowlist hook and fixed prompt rules. - Closure path: no identity issue→legitimate authority→returned authoritative policy path found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: initial requirements and tool authorization are not runtime identity governance. - Evidence: client.py, security.py, prompts/codingprompt.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded source inspection. - Caveats: Static supported-path assessment; no live model/provider execution or effectiveness claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3b92b0844ae8313e11e6ade32409a9d1d9eca54a/assessments/li/linear-coding-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3b92b0844ae8313e11e6ade32409a9d1d9eca54a\">3b92b08 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T22:55:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/code-yeongyu/oh-my-openagent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">oh-my-openagent</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/code-yeongyu/oh-my-openagent/tree/879a8b791e511edee68bada80187e0febb9b1aac\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 879a8b791e511edee68bada80187e0febb9b1aac\">879a8b7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. Team members are built-in autonomous agents that claim assigned or available work and execute it in their own worktrees. Their model/tool loops produce the operational outcomes of the team.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: A. Team Mode closes a concrete coordination loop among distinct S1s: atomic task claims prevent duplicate ownership, dependency gates suppress premature work, isolated member worktrees attenuate concurrent file interference, and the lead/member agents autonomously react to blockers and task availability. This is more than a mailbox or delegation graph because the mechanisms change later worker behavior in response to shared interference state.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: A. The Team Lead has a whole-team view of tasks, messages and member status and is instructed to assign/reassign work, resolve blockers, manage member lifecycle, monitor progress and own project-level completion before synthesis. Those current-control decisions are made by a first-party autonomous lead agent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. Specialist/reviewer-shaped agents exist, but the reviewed Team Mode evidence does not establish a materially independent audit channel with distinct access and corrective closure over the operating team.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. Research/delegation specialists gather information for current work; no distinct outside-looking prospective organizational adaptation loop was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. User prompts, agent definitions and team instructions provide operating goals and role constraints, not an ultimate organizational identity/policy tension-and-resolution loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9b431dfc4e818567d073e9a545bf346497b4addd/assessments/oh-my-openagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9b431dfc4e818567d073e9a545bf346497b4addd\">9b431df &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T20:14:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tale-project/tale\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tale</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tale-project/tale/tree/b2560c2d7d3294993e66129c891be4a059f3d6c4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b2560c2d7d3294993e66129c891be4a059f3d6c4\">b2560c2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce project task deliverables through agent-owned execution of assigned task briefs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous user requests, files and tool observations, provider responses, task feedback, source changes and execution failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: within an authorized turn, the configured autonomous agent chooses substantive task actions, tool interactions, corrections and the output to return. - Decision owner: configured model-driven project-agent actor in Tale&#39;s supported project-agent run; Tale does not claim first-party ownership of the selected external runtime&#39;s internal agent loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task assignment, task brief/context injection, sandbox session/worker, credential/tool bridge, output harvesting, run deadlines and agent retry. - Closure path: task brief and current project context → selected agent turn in its sandbox → model-driven actions/tool observations → updated actions and deliverables → persisted task report/artifacts and reviewer feedback → subsequent task work. - Boundary reachability: normal project-agent task starts create a standing sandbox turn for a configured supported harness; the implementation and task tools are shipped with Tale. No contributor-only executor is needed, though a configured external agent runtime/provider is a declared operational dependency. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the configured agent actor leaves task/sandbox/queue infrastructure but no substantive choice of task actions or deliverable; the host does not decide content. - Evidence: run host launch and settle; tool bridge task actions; README supported runtimes. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for the supported composed agent-run path; no claim about autonomous management on a default unconfigured install. - Caveats: model providers and selectable external agent runtimes are dependencies. Their own S2-S5 are not inherited by Tale. A describes the reachable assembled S1 actor, not an autonomous Tale-owned model implementation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate operational interference between concurrent project-agent work cells through isolated workers, per-task single-live-run admission and capacity-aware waiting. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two attempted runs on the same task, multiple task turns sharing a worker&#39;s files, and agents competing for sandbox host execution capacity. - Distinct S1 units: separate project-agent task runs, potentially simultaneous on distinct workers/tasks. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping starts would collide on task ownership/review state; reused shared workspaces risk stale file contamination, while shared host capacity can cause execution contention. - Attenuating coordination relation: first-party task-run admission/status guards and worker-scoped workspace/delivery box, capacity queues and optional host heavy-phase leases. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: denied/already-running starts do not spawn another worker; queued starts wait for available capacity; a settled turn frees its worker slot, allowing later authorized work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the paths target explicit concurrent-run/cross-task file/host-contention disturbances, not merely task handoff or a mailbox. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide that conflicting starts should be refused or capacity-parked and that isolation/leases should govern subsequent starts. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic platform safety and admission rules; no Tale agent is established as owning a discretionary project-wide S2 coordination response in the credited base mode. This is a bounded S2-specific constructor/coordination path rather than A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: transactional run gates, standing worker allocation, task-specific output boxes, capacity retries and host lease tools. - Closure path: competing/oversubscribed start → guarded admission, isolated worker or capacity park → next start resumes only after resource/turn clearance. - Boundary reachability: ordinary shipped task-run/worker path; no extension-only coordinator is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: changing platform admission rules requires developer/operator composition; the configured work agent does not choose the S2 collision policy. C is credited to its concrete S2-specific path, not generic ability to send messages. - Evidence: standing worker isolation; task-specific delivery box; host lease admission; start tool occupancy semantics. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: mechanical isolation and exclusive-run rules do not imply that a model agent owns coordination discretion; host leases alone are not an organizational S2 decision.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current project-wide task priorities, assigned operational commitments and intervention/review handling across multiple agent work cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing unfinished tasks, changing assignments, blocked reviews, stale execution/review state and limited project-agent capacity. - Whole-system current view: project/board-wide taskfind lists task status, priority/assignee and captured review; taskget exposes task dependencies, current run and pending review. - Current-control decision scope: choose priority/agent assignment, start/resume another qualified agent, redirect a captured review to a different eligible independent agent, and decide which work receives next attention; operator task views/actions can exercise parent control separately. - Decisive decision or feedback right: project-wide assignment/priority and review-intervention choices based on current task portfolio, beyond one task&#39;s own model/tool steps. - Decision owner: base mode — explicitly granted manager agent with project-wide task authority; parent mode — legitimate project operator/editor acting through normal board/project task controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scoped tool grants, expected-value/CAS task mutations, current run identity, protected review transfer, capacity checks and status writes. - Closure path: manager&#39;s project task view → discretionary priority/assignment/review-transfer choice → guarded platform write → later agent starts/reviews use revised state; alternatively the human project operator chooses and commits an equivalent update through the UI/API. - Boundary reachability: Tale ships supported task-find/get/update/start/delegate-review tools for project agents; manager rights must be explicitly granted in the project. Direct operator task management is a first-party platform mode. Neither is a repository-maintainer or adjacent dogfood actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in manager mode, remove the manager&#39;s judgment and no project-wide choice of which task/agent/review to prioritize remains, only platform guards. In parent mode the operator owns that choice; neither platform lock nor scheduler is itself the decision owner. - Evidence: project-wide task observation; manager priority, assignment and review delegation; native task writes and actor paths; project task review and task UI. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for optional configured manager and project operator modes. - Caveats: having a manager label or an automatic scheduler alone is not evidence. The decisive right here is the explicitly scoped live manager&#39;s discretionary whole-project task-choice path. Ordinary operator approval of one output does not supply the S3 parent claim.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge an agent-produced task completion claim using source-bound artifacts and a distinct reviewer agent, returning a corrective or approving decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: misleading or stale self-reports, missing/inadequate output evidence, self-review conflicts, and acceptance claims unsupported by the actual task artifacts. - Claim being audited: the implementation agent&#39;s settled run and current task evidence support completion/acceptance. - Ordinary reporting path: implementation run&#39;s task comment and harvested deliverables enter inreview; the implementing agent cannot move its own task to done. - Complementary access path: a designated distinct review agent can read the captured source run, access available input/output files via guarded stagefile, perform independent checks and submit structured check/PR evidence. - Independence boundary: the reviewer must differ from implementationAgentId, must have the captured pending review/grant and matching evidence revision; human-competence and human-only gates cannot be bypassed. - Who acts on findings: the reviewer agent itself chooses approve/requestchanges. A serializable review transaction atomically moves the task to done or back to todo, posts feedback and records the decision; a separate implementation agent can then repair the task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judging the result of a separate operational run from independently accessed artifacts rather than accepting worker status. - Decision owner: authorized distinct reviewing model agent; platform validates source identity and commits the verdict but does not choose approve/requestchanges. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: source/evidence-revision CAS, staged files, structured checks, independent reviewer-ID guard, native approval record and audit log. - Closure path: settled worker run → captured evidence and independent reviewer access → reviewer verdict + explanation → guarded task status/feedback transaction → acceptance or a subsequent correction run. - Boundary reachability: a native first-party project-agent review mode is shipped; the reviewer must be separately granted the task-review tool and assigned an eligible captured review. This is not Tale OSS CI and not merely the worker&#39;s own routine tests. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the reviewing agent leaves a schema/permission gate but no independent verdict on artifacts. The worker cannot impersonate the independent reviewer; the model reviewer chooses the corrective judgment. - Evidence: captured source and distinct reviewer validation; native verdict and returned task status; review file staging and reviewer tool contract. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for the supported independently assigned agent-review mode. - Caveats: the server cannot independently verify the substantive truth of every reviewer-supplied check, and native review is not necessarily a default path for every project. This positive finding relies on a separate authorized review agent examining actual evidence, not on the deterministic verdict parser or generic review label.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no distinct external-and-prospective project capability adaptation loop is established beyond task work and current automation/operational controls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tale agents can research outside information for their assigned tasks and automation can schedule later runs, but these do not alone generate and return a project-level future capability strategy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no supported first-party S4-level prospective adaptation authority and return into capability is established. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task research/web tools, project history, automation editors/schedules, project instruction editing, agent/model configuration, recurring tasks and task-level planning. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model carrying out a foresight research task is S1 unless its result enters an established project capability-adaptation conversation. A schedule or prompt change does not itself close that distinct loop. - Evidence: task tool catalog; platform automations; project instructions. - Basis: structural negative search. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: operators could compose future planning and adaptation tasks, but a hypothetical configured workflow is not a shipped independent S4 closure. No claim is made that future implementations cannot supply one.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: establish durable project-purpose and governing instructions that subsequent project-agent operation must obey. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project goal drift, incompatible instructions, unauthorized scope changes, and changes to the owner&#39;s project-level operating rules. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: what the project is for, what instructions/constraints its agents operate under, and which rules are authoritative across future task runs. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: a legitimate authorized project owner/editor changes project instructions; a task worker or manager is not shown to possess a shipped autonomous right to redefine this project identity without separately granted parent authority. - Return-to-operation path: updated project instructions are persisted in the project record and read into the agent-run task context for later work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: approve and persist the project&#39;s authoritative durable instructions. - Decision owner: authorized parent project editor/operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project permissions, expected hash and audit logging, durable project table, mandatory context-injection helpers, run instruction snapshot. - Closure path: project identity/instruction change proposed → authorized editor chooses content → version-checked persistent update → later task-agent launch reads the project instructions and the agent&#39;s work is governed by them. - Boundary reachability: first-party supported project configuration/update operation and standard run preparation. No contributor governance or unshipped policy agent is credited. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent task tools can act within granted scope but do not by themselves select legitimate ultimate project-purpose changes; the parent editor holds the decision while the platform records and returns it. A task-level approval and static safety filter alone would not establish S5. - Evidence: authorized persistent project-instruction update; task-run instruction capture; project agent instructions. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: this parent mode concerns project purpose/operating identity, not all platform-wide security policy or the Tale repository&#39;s maintainer decisions. No internal autonomous S5 decision path is credited.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3d84f95617c8dfaa9fad883bc20aaad7cbd9922a/assessments/ta/tale.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3d84f95617c8dfaa9fad883bc20aaad7cbd9922a\">3d84f95 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
